irus RNA was also found in the patient’s serum, stool samples, and muscle biopsy.”\n- No direct match in Predicted Diagnostic Reasons. The predicted reasons do not mention enterovirus infection or any specific microbiological/virological findings. They focus on the general inflammatory myopathy and its association with ibrutinib.\n\nSummary of final matching decisions:\n- Groundtruth 1: No match.\n- Groundtruth 2: No match.\n- Groundtruth 3: Matches with \"The patient's symptoms of muscle pain, swelling, and elevated CK levels, along with the muscle biopsy findings, are consistent with an inflammatory myopathy. The temporal association with ibrutinib use and the lack of response to discontinuation of the medication suggest a possible association with the drug. The presence of interstitial and perivascular T-cell and macrophage infiltrates on muscle biopsy also supports this diagnosis.\" and \"The patient's symptoms of muscle pain, swelling, and elevated CK levels, along with the muscle biopsy findings, are consistent with an inflammatory myopathy. The temporal association with ibrutinib use and the lack of response to discontinuation of the medication suggest a possible association with the drug.\" because both mention the lack of response to discontinuation of the medication, which is a key part of the groundtruth statement regarding therapy-related side effects.\n- Groundtruth 4: No match.\n- Groundtruth 5: No match.\n- Groundtruth 6: No match. The predicted reasons are too general (\"inflammatory myopathy\", \"myositis\") to specifically match \"viral myositis\".\n- Groundtruth 7: No match.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [\n      \"The patient's symptoms of muscle pain, swelling, and elevated CK levels, along with the muscle biopsy findings, are consistent with an inflammatory myopathy. The temporal association with ibrutinib use and the lack of response to discontinuation of the medication suggest a possible association with the drug. The presence of interstitial and perivascular T-cell and macrophage infiltrates on muscle biopsy also supports this diagnosis.\",\n      \"The patient's symptoms of muscle pain, swelling, and elevated CK levels, along with the muscle biopsy findings, are consistent with an inflammatory myopathy. The temporal association with ibrutinib use and the lack of response to discontinuation of the medication suggest a possible association with the drug.\"\n    ],\n    \"4\": [],\n    \"5\": [],\n    \"6\": [],\n    \"7\": []\n  }\n}\n```\n</diagnostic_comparison>",
