-centromere antibodies and the specific pattern of organ involvement make Systemic Sclerosis a more likely diagnosis.\"\n    - Pros: Mentions \"specific pattern of organ involvement\" which could implicitly include pulmonary hypertension.\n    - Cons: Too general, does not specifically mention pulmonary hypertension or the details of the initial PAH diagnosis.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 2: Rheumatoid arthritis was considered given the polyarthritis and joint effusions but was excluded because “rheumatoid arthritis (RA) tests were negative.”\n- No direct mention of Rheumatoid Arthritis or its exclusion in the predicted reasons. The predicted reasons discuss polyarthritis and joint swelling in the context of Systemic Sclerosis and Mixed Connective Tissue Disease, but not RA specifically.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 3: Limited or diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis was considered but excluded on the basis of no skin changes—“The main diagnostic criteria are the absence of skin thickening….”\n- No direct mention of the exclusion of limited or diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis due to the absence of skin changes in the predicted reasons. The predicted reasons mention \"Systemic Sclerosis (Limited Cutaneous Scleroderma)\" as a diagnosis, but not its exclusion based on skin changes.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 4: Systemic sclerosis sine scleroderma was established when internal organ involvement, Raynaud’s phenomenon, positive anti-centromere antibodies, and the absence of another connective tissue disease were recognized—“The presence of pulmonary hypertension, Raynaud’s phenomenon, positive anti-centromere antibodies, and the exclusion of another defined connective tissue disease confirmed the diagnosis of systemic sclerosis sine scleroderma.”\n- Potential match 1: \"The patient's symptoms, including polyarthritis, joint swelling, Raynaud's phenomenon, and anti-centromere antibody positivity, are consistent with Systemic Sclerosis. The presence of pulm