tern of lung involvement on chest CT make Systemic Sclerosis a more likely diagnosis.\" (Covers Raynaud's, anti-centromere, and exclusion of MCTD)\n- \"The patient's presentation with polyarthritis, joint swelling, and elevated inflammatory markers, along with the presence of anti-centromere antibodies, could also suggest Mixed Connective Tissue Disease. However, the presence of idiopathic pulmonary hypertension and specific echocardiography findings make Systemic Sclerosis a more likely diagnosis.\" (Covers anti-centromere, pulmonary hypertension, and exclusion of MCTD)\n- \"The patient's clinical presentation, including polyarthritis, joint swelling, and positive antinuclear antibody, could also be consistent with Mixed Connective Tissue Disease. However, the presence of anti-centromere antibodies and the specific pattern of organ involvement make Systemic Sclerosis a more likely diagnosis.\" (Covers anti-centromere and exclusion of MCTD)\n\nAll predicted reasons contribute to the final diagnosis of Systemic Sclerosis by highlighting key features (Raynaud's, anti-centromere, pulmonary hypertension) and/or by differentiating it from other connective tissue diseases (MCTD), thus implicitly addressing the \"exclusion of another defined connective tissue disease.\"\n\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [\n      \"The patient's presentation with polyarthritis, joint swelling, and elevated inflammatory markers, along with the presence of anti-centromere antibodies, could also suggest Mixed Connective Tissue Disease. However, the presence of idiopathic pulmonary hypertension and specific echocardiography findings make Systemic Sclerosis a more likely diagnosis.\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": [\n      \"The patient's symptoms, including polyarthritis, joint swelling, Raynaud's phenomenon, and anti-centromere antibody positivity, are consistent with Systemic Sclerosis. The presence of pulmonary hypertension and i