al matching decisions:\n- Groundtruth 1: No match.\n- Groundtruth 2: No match.\n- Groundtruth 3: All predicted statements supporting Peutz-Jeghers Syndrome are considered matches because they collectively demonstrate that the diagnosis is based on a combination of hamartomatous polyps, pigmentation (brownish macules, hyperchromic punctate lesions), and other clinical criteria (symptoms, lab findings, endoscopic details), which is the essence of the groundtruth statement.\n- Groundtruth 4: No match.\n- Groundtruth 5: No match.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [\n      \"The patient's presentation of watery diarrhea, vomiting, abdominal pain, and significant weight loss, combined with the physical examination findings of brownish macules on the hands and feet and hyperchromic punctate lesions on the trunk, is suggestive of a gastrointestinal disorder. The laboratory studies demonstrating hypoproteinemia, hypoalbuminemia, zinc deficiency, and low vitamin B12 levels further support this diagnosis. The endoscopic findings of multiple type II and III Yamada polyps on atrophic gastric mucosa and nodular lymphoid lesions in the duodenum, as well as numerous sessile and subpedunculated polyps throughout the colon and rectum, are consistent with Peutz-Jeghers Syndrome, a rare autosomal dominant disorder characterized by the development of hamartomatous polyps in the gastrointestinal tract and an increased risk of certain cancers.\",\n      \"The patient's presentation of diffuse abdominal pain, watery diarrhea, and weight loss, combined with the physical examination findings of brownish macules on the hands and feet and hyperchromic punctate lesions on the trunk, are suggestive of Peutz-Jeghers Syndrome. The presence of multiple type II and III Yamada polyps on atrophic gastric mucosa and nodular lymphoid lesions in the duodenum, as well as numerous sessile and subpedunculated polyps throughout the colon and rectum, further sup