    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Corneal melanoma was considered because of the presence of pigmented stromal lesions — “Corneal pigmented lesions could also be caused by corneal melanoma or corneal melanin deposition caused by topical epinephrine therapy.”\n2. Corneal melanin deposition from topical epinephrine was considered for the same reason — “Corneal pigmented lesions could also be caused by corneal melanoma or corneal melanin deposition caused by topical epinephrine therapy.”\n3. Prior pine processionary infection was evaluated as a potential source but deemed unlikely — “Despite the patient's history of Thaumetopoea pityocampa infection, this is an unlikely source, due to no association with Exophiala xenobiotica being reported and the absence of infection reported at the time of DSAEK.”\n4. Keratitis by more common Exophiala species was considered given previous reports but excluded when culture identified a rare strain — “Few cases of keratitis caused by Exophiala spp. have been reported: E. dermatitidis keratitis after keratoplasty and after laser in situ keratomileusis, E. jeanselmei keratitis after minor trauma and soft contact lens use, and E. phaeomuriformis keratitis after severe ocular surface problems,” … “Diagnosis was established by a positive direct examination and culture (Figure 3) of the same fungus from the surgically obtained corneal button.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\nDiagnosis: Fungal keratitis\nEvidence: The patient's history of an eye infection and the presence of brown, multilobular lesions in the deep corneal stroma, as seen on slit-lamp biomicroscopy, are suggestive of fungal keratitis. The lack of conjunctival hyperemia, discharge, and epithelial defect also supports this diagnosis, as fungal keratitis can present with a relatively quiet eye.\nEvidence: The patient's history of eye infection and the presence of brown, multilobular lesions in the deep corneal stroma, as well as the central infiltrate involving all corneal layers on optical coherence tomography, are consistent with fungal keratitis. The lack of conjunctival hyperemia, discharge, and epithelial defect also suggests a fungal etiology.\nEvidence: The patient's history of eye infection and the presence of brown, multilobular lesions in the deep corneal stroma, as seen on slit-lamp biomicroscopy, are suggestive of fungal keratitis. The lack of conjunctival hyperemia, discharge, and epithelial defect also supports this diagnosis, as fungal keratitis can present with a relatively quiet eye.\nDiagnosis: Nocardia keratitis\nEvidence: The patient's history of prior eye infection and the presence of multilobular lesions in the deep corneal stroma, as well as the findings on anterior segment optical coherence tomography, could also be consistent with Nocardia keratitis. However, the absence of a clear history of trauma or exposure to soil or organic matter makes this diagnosis less likely than fungal keratitis.\nEvidence: The patient's history of corticosteroid use and the presence of multilobular lesions in the deep corneal stroma could also be consistent with Nocardia keratitis. However, the absence of a clear history of trauma or exposure to soil or water, which are common risk factors for Nocardia infection, makes this diagnosis less likely than fungal keratitis.\n\nAnalysis of potential matches:\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 1: Corneal melanoma was considered because of the presence of pigmented stromal lesions — “Corneal pigmented lesions could also be caused by corneal melanoma or corneal melanin deposition caused by topical epinephrine therapy.”\n\n*   **Potential Match 1:** \"The patient's history of an eye infection and the presence of brown, multilobular lesions in the deep corneal stroma, as seen on slit-lamp biomicroscopy, are suggestive of fungal keratitis.\"\n    *   **Pros:** Mentions \"brown, multilobular lesions in the deep corneal stroma,\" which aligns with \"pigmented 