    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Liver adenoma was initially considered given prior biopsies—“The primary differential diagnosis was bleeding episode of liver adenoma—misled by the previously obtained histopathology report.”\n2. Hepatocellular carcinoma was suspected because of persistent hypervascular lesions with necrosis—“Because of a suspected atypical hepatocellular carcinoma, an additional magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) study was performed … and a surgical resection was indicated.”\n3. Hepatic-origin tumors were excluded by immunohistochemistry negative for liver markers—“Immunohistochemical staining revealed … and lack of reaction against … Arginase-1, for exclusion of a liver-own tumor.”\n4. Metastatic liver tumor and intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma were considered in adults with hypervascular hepatic masses—“In adults its differential diagnoses can be metastatic liver tumor or intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma.”\n5. Liver hematoma was part of the differential given imaging evidence of hemorrhage—“Preoperative CT-scan … showing hypervascularized hepatic lesions with central necrosis and associated hematoma.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\nDiagnosis: Hepatic Adenoma with Hemorrhage\nEvidence: The patient has a history of hypervascular liver lesions in segment IV, which were biopsied and shown to be adenomas. The current presentation with acute-onset abdominal pain, anemia, and imaging findings of subcapsular hepatic bleeding and active bleeding from one of the lesions is consistent with hemorrhage from a hepatic adenoma.\nDiagnosis: Atypical Hepatocellular Carcinoma\nEvidence: Although the initial biopsies showed adenomas without malignancy, the persistence of hypervascular lesions and the development of central necrosis on MRI raise suspicion for an atypical hepatocellular carcinoma. The planned elective surgical resection is also consistent with this diagnosis.\nEvidence: The patient's lesions have atypical features, including central necrosis on MRI, which raises suspicion for an atypical hepatocellular carcinoma. The lack of typical features of hepatocellular carcinoma on CT and normal tumor marker levels do not rule out this diagnosis, and the planned elective surgical resection is likely aimed at obtaining a definitive diagnosis.\nEvidence: The patient's lesions have atypical features, including hypervascularity and central necrosis on MRI, which raises suspicion for an atypical hepatocellular carcinoma. The lack of typical features of hepatocellular carcinoma on CT and normal tumor marker levels do not entirely rule out this diagnosis, and the planned surgical resection is likely aimed at obtaining a definitive diagnosis.\n\nAnalysis of potential matches:\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 1: Liver adenoma was initially considered given prior biopsies—“The primary differential diagnosis was bleeding episode of liver adenoma—misled by the previously obtained histopathology report.”\n- Potential match 1: \"The patient has a history of hypervascular liver lesions in segment IV, which were biopsied and shown to be adenomas. The current presentation with acute-onset abdominal pain, anemia, and imaging findings of subcapsular hepatic bleeding and active bleeding from one of the lesions is consistent with hemorrhage from a hepatic adenoma.\"\n    - Pros: This statement directly mentions \"adenomas\" and \"biopsied,\" aligning with the ground truth's reference to \"prior biopsies\" and \"liver adenoma.\" It also mentions \"bleeding\" which is consistent with \"bleeding episode of liver adenoma.\"\n    - Cons: None.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 2: Hepatocellular carcinoma was suspected because of persistent hypervascular lesions with necrosis—“Because of a suspected atypical hepatocellular carcinoma, an additional magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) study was performed … and a surgical resection was indicated.”\n- Potential match 1: \"Although the initial biopsies showed adenomas