  "edb8e371042831ac0d42f61e598a0eede99c7cc1006948889773d79b6f3ec05f": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Connective tissue– or vasculitis‐associated ILD was excluded — “Connective tissue disease‐associated interstitial lung disease (ILD) and vasculitis‐associated ILD were deemed unlikely based on the negative autoantibodies results in serological testing…”\n2. Hypersensitivity pneumonitis was ruled out — “hypersensitivity pneumonitis was considered unlikely due to the absence of any identifiable history of antigen exposure.”\n3. Bacterial pneumonia was considered and excluded when antibiotics failed — “the patient was subsequently treated with lascufloxacin, followed by tazobactam/ceftolozane, but showed no improvement in oxygen saturation and worsening respiratory status…”\n4. SARS‐CoV‐2 infection was excluded — “SARS‐CoV‐2 polymerase chain reaction were negative.”\n5. Malignant infiltration was ruled out by biopsy — “Pathological examination of the trans‐bronchial lung biopsy specimen revealed no malignant findings…”\n6. Infection was further excluded by negative BAL cultures — “culture tests of the lower respiratory tract specimens obtained during bronchoscopy were negative for pathogens.”\n7. ICI-induced pneumonitis was suspected based on timing and imaging — “Based on these clinical findings, ICI‐induced pneumonitis (Grade 3) was suspected.”\n8. Steroid refractoriness was established by persistent infiltrates and worsened oxygen requirement despite two high‐dose steroid courses — “radiographic findings taken after these treatments showed a persistent infiltrative shadow, and the patient required increased oxygen support, with the flow rate rising from 2 to 4 L/min,” and “no significant changes in the respiratory status of the patient.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n- The patient developed new bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT after receiving ipilimumab and nivolumab, and had symptoms of fever and dyspnea, which are consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis. The lack of response to antibiotics and the presence of lymphocytosis in the bronchoalveolar lavage fluid also support this diagnosis.\n- The patient developed new bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT after receiving ipilimumab and nivolumab, and had symptoms such as fever and dyspnea, which are consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis. The lack of response to antibiotics and the presence of lymphocytosis in the bronchoalveolar lavage fluid also support this diagnosis.\n- The patient's symptoms of fever, dyspnea, and bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT, combined with the lack of response to antibiotics and the presence of lymphocytic predominance in bronchoalveolar lavage fluid, are consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis. The patient's history of treatment with ipilimumab and nivolumab also supports this diagnosis.\n- The patient's clinical presentation, laboratory results, and imaging findings are consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis, which is a known side effect of immunotherapy with ipilimumab and nivolumab. The lack of response to steroid treatment and the results of the bronchoalveolar lavage fluid and transbronchial lung biopsy support this diagnosis.\n- The patient did not respond to high-dose methylprednisolone, which is a common treatment for ICI-induced pneumonitis, and his oxygen requirements continued to increase, indicating a severe and refractory disease course.\n- The patient's lack of response to high-dose methylprednisolone and prednisolone, as evidenced by the persistence of symptoms and unchanged chest radiographs, suggests steroid-refractory disease. The bronchoalveolar lavage fluid findings and transbronchial lung biopsy results also support this diagnosis.\n\nAnalysis of potential matches:\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 1: Connective tissue– or vasculitis‐associated ILD was excluded — “Connective tissue disease‐associated interstitial lung disease (ILD) and vasculitis‐associated ILD were deemed unlikely based on the negative autoantibodies results in serological testing…”\n- No direct match in Predicted Diagnostic Reasons. The predicted reasons focus on supporting ICI-induced pneumonitis and steroid refractoriness, not on ruling out other specific conditions like CTD/vasculitis-associated ILD.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 2: Hypersensitivity pneumonitis was ruled out — “hypersensitivity pneumonitis was considered unlikely due to the absence of any identifiable history of antigen exposure.”\n- No direct match in Predicted Diagnostic Reasons. Similar to GR1, the predicted reasons do not mention ruling out hypersensitivity pneumonitis.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 3: Bacterial pneumonia was considered and excluded when antibiotics failed — “the patient was subsequently treated with lascufloxacin, followed by tazobactam/ceftolozane, but showed no improvement in oxygen saturation and worsening respiratory status…”\n- Potential match 1: \"The patient developed new bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT after receiving ipilimumab and nivolumab, and had symptoms of fever and dyspnea, which are consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis. The lack of response to antibiotics and the presence of lymphocytosis in the bronchoalveolar lavage fluid also support this diagnosis.\"\n    - Pros: Explicitly mentions \"lack of response to antibiotics.\"\n    - Cons: Does not specify bacterial pneumonia or the types of antibiotics used. However, \"lack of response to antibiotics\" strongly implies that bacterial infection was considered and ruled out by treatment failure.\n- Potential match 2: \"The patient developed new bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT after receiving ipilimumab and nivolumab, and had symptoms such as fever and dyspnea, which are consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis. The lack of response to antibiotics and the presence of lymphocytosis in the bronchoalveolar lavage fluid also support this diagnosis.\"\n    - Pros: Identical to Potential match 1, explicitly mentions \"lack of response to antibiotics.\"\n    - Cons: Same as above.\n- Potential match 3: \"The patient's symptoms of fever, dyspnea, and bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT, combined with the lack of response to antibiotics and the presence of lymphocytic predominance in bronchoalveolar lavage fluid, are consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis. The patient's history of treatment with ipilimumab and nivolumab also supports this diagnosis.\"\n    - Pros: Explicitly mentions \"lack of response to antibiotics.\"\n    - Cons: Same as above.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 4: SARS‐CoV‐2 infection was excluded — “SARS‐CoV‐2 polymerase chain reaction were negative.”\n- No direct match in Predicted Diagnostic Reasons. The predicted reasons do not mention SARS-CoV-2 exclusion.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 5: Malignant infiltration was ruled out by biopsy — “Pathological examination of the trans‐bronchial lung biopsy specimen revealed no malignant findings…”\n- Potential match 1: \"The patient's clinical presentation, laboratory results, and imaging findings are consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis, which is a known side effect of immunotherapy with ipilimumab and nivolumab. The lack of response to steroid treatment and the results of the bronchoalveolar lavage fluid and transbronchial lung biopsy support this diagnosis.\"\n    - Pros: Mentions \"transbronchial lung biopsy\" as supporting the diagnosis. While it doesn't explicitly state \"no malignant findings,\" the biopsy result supporting ICI-induced pneumonitis implicitly means other diagnoses like malignancy were ruled out.\n    - Cons: Does not explicitly state \"no malignant findings.\"\n- Potential match 2: \"The patient's lack of response to high-dose methylprednisolone and prednisolone, as evidenced by the persistence of symptoms and unchanged chest radiographs, suggests steroid-refractory disease. The bronchoalveolar lavage fluid findings and transbronchial lung biopsy results also support this diagnosis.\"\n    - Pros: Mentions \"transbronchial lung biopsy results\" as supporting the diagnosis.\n    - Cons: Does not explicitly state \"no malignant findings.\"\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 6: Infection was further excluded by negative BAL cultures — “culture tests of the lower respiratory tract specimens obtained during bronchoscopy were negative for pathogens.”\n- Potential match 1: \"The patient developed new bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT after receiving ipilimumab and nivolumab, and had symptoms of fever and dyspnea, which are consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis. The lack of response to antibiotics and the presence of lymphocytosis in the bronchoalveolar lavage fluid also support this diagnosis.\"\n    - Pros: Mentions \"lymphocytosis in the bronchoalveolar lavage fluid\" which is a finding from BAL. While not explicitly stating \"negative cultures,\" the focus on lymphocytosis as supporting ICI-pneumonitis implies other causes (like bacterial infection) were less likely.\n    - Cons: Does not explicitly state \"negative BAL cultures for pathogens.\"\n- Potential match 2: \"The patient developed new bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT after receiving ipilimumab and nivolumab, and had symptoms such as fever and dyspnea, which are consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis. The lack of response to antibiotics and the presence of lymphocytosis in the bronchoalveolar lavage fluid also support this diagnosis.\"\n    - Pros: Identical to Potential match 1.\n    - Cons: Same as above.\n- Potential match 3: \"The patient's symptoms of fever, dyspnea, and bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT, combined with the lack of response to antibiotics and the presence of lymphocytic predominance in bronchoalveolar lavage fluid, are consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis. The patient's history of treatment with ipilimumab and nivolumab also supports this diagnosis.\"\n    - Pros: Mentions \"lymphocytic predominance in bronchoalveolar lavage fluid.\"\n    - Cons: Does not explicitly state \"negative BAL cultures for pathogens.\"\n- Potential match 4: \"The patient's clinical presentation, laboratory results, and imaging findings are consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis, which is a known side effect of immunotherapy with ipilimumab and nivolumab. The lack of response to steroid treatment and the results of the bronchoalveolar lavage fluid and transbronchial lung biopsy support this diagnosis.\"\n    - Pros: Mentions \"results of the bronchoalveolar lavage fluid\" supporting the diagnosis.\n    - Cons: Does not explicitly state \"negative BAL cultures for pathogens.\"\n- Potential match 5: \"The patient's lack of response to high-dose methylprednisolone and prednisolone, as evidenced by the persistence of symptoms and unchanged chest radiographs, suggests steroid-refractory disease. The bronchoalveolar lavage fluid findings and transbronchial lung biopsy results also support this diagnosis.\"\n    - Pros: Mentions \"bronchoalveolar lavage fluid findings\" supporting the diagnosis.\n    - Cons: Does not explicitly state \"negative BAL cultures for pathogens.\"\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 7: ICI-induced pneumonitis was suspected based on timing and imaging — “Based on these clinical findings, ICI‐induced pneumonitis (Grade 3) was suspected.”\n- Potential match 1: \"The patient developed new bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT after receiving ipilimumab and nivolumab, and had symptoms of fever and dyspnea, which are consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis. The lack of response to antibiotics and the presence of lymphocytosis in the bronchoalveolar lavage fluid also support this diagnosis.\"\n    - Pros: Mentions \"new bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT after receiving ipilimumab and nivolumab\" and \"symptoms of fever and dyspnea,\" all consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis. This aligns with \"timing and imaging.\"\n    - Cons: None.\n- Potential match 2: \"The patient developed new bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT after receiving ipilimumab and nivolumab, and had symptoms such as fever and dyspnea, which are consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis. The lack of response to antibiotics and the presence of lymphocytosis in the bronchoalveolar lavage fluid also support this diagnosis.\"\n    - Pros: Identical to Potential match 1.\n    - Cons: None.\n- Potential match 3: \"The patient's symptoms of fever, dyspnea, and bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT, combined with the lack of response to antibiotics and the presence of lymphocytic predominance in bronchoalveolar lavage fluid, are consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis. The patient's history of treatment with ipilimumab and nivolumab also supports this diagnosis.\"\n    - Pros: Mentions \"symptoms of fever, dyspnea, and bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT\" and \"history of treatment with ipilimumab and nivolumab,\" which directly relates to timing and imaging in the context of ICI.\n    - Cons: None.\n- Potential match 4: \"The patient's clinical presentation, laboratory results, and imaging findings are consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis, which is a known side effect of immunotherapy with ipilimumab and nivolumab. The lack of response to steroid treatment and the results of the bronchoalveolar lavage fluid and transbronchial lung biopsy support this diagnosis.\"\n    - Pros: Mentions \"clinical presentation, laboratory results, and imaging findings are consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis\" and \"known side effect of immunotherapy with ipilimumab and nivolumab.\" This covers timing (after immunotherapy) and imaging.\n    - Cons: None.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 8: Steroid refractoriness was established by persistent infiltrates and worsened oxygen requirement despite two high‐dose steroid courses — “radiographic findings taken after these treatments showed a persistent infiltrative shadow, and the patient required increased oxygen support, with the flow rate rising from 2 to 4 L/min,” and “no significant changes in the respiratory status of the patient.”\n- Potential match 1: \"The patient's clinical presentation, laboratory results, and imaging findings are consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis, which is a known side effect of immunotherapy with ipilimumab and nivolumab. The lack of response to steroid treatment and the results of the bronchoalveolar lavage fluid and transbronchial lung biopsy support this diagnosis.\"\n    - Pros: Explicitly states \"The lack of response to steroid treatment.\"\n    - Cons: Does not detail the persistent infiltrates or worsened oxygen requirement.\n- Potential match 2: \"The patient did not respond to high-dose methylprednisolone, which is a common treatment for ICI-induced pneumonitis, and his oxygen requirements continued to increase, indicating a severe and refractory disease course.\"\n    - Pros: Explicitly states \"did not respond to high-dose methylprednisolone\" and \"oxygen requirements continued to increase,\" directly matching the groundtruth.\n    - Cons: Does not explicitly mention \"persistent infiltrates\" but \"did not respond\" and \"oxygen requirements continued to increase\" strongly imply lack of improvement in lung status.\n- Potential match 3: \"The patient's lack of response to high-dose methylprednisolone and prednisolone, as evidenced by the persistence of symptoms and unchanged chest radiographs, suggests steroid-refractory disease. The bronchoalveolar lavage fluid findings and transbronchial lung biopsy results also support this diagnosis.\"\n    - Pros: Explicitly states \"lack of response to high-dose methylprednisolone and prednisolone,\" \"persistence of symptoms,\" and \"unchanged chest radiographs,\" which directly aligns with \"persistent infiltrates\" and \"no significant changes in the respiratory status.\"\n    - Cons: Does not explicitly mention \"worsened oxygen requirement\" but \"persistence of symptoms\" and \"unchanged chest radiographs\" are strong indicators of refractoriness.\n\nSummary of final matching decisions:\n\nGR1: No direct match.\nGR2: No direct match.\nGR3: Matches with the three predicted statements that mention \"lack of response to antibiotics.\"\nGR4: No direct match.\nGR5: Matches with the two predicted statements that mention \"transbronchial lung biopsy\" as supporting the diagnosis, as ruling out malignancy is an implicit part of using biopsy to support ICI-pneumonitis.\nGR6: Matches with the five predicted statements that mention \"bronchoalveolar lavage fluid\" or \"lymphocytosis/lymphocytic predominance in bronchoalveolar lavage fluid\" as supporting the diagnosis, as negative cultures are part of the BAL workup to exclude infection.\nGR7: Matches with the four predicted statements that describe the clinical presentation (symptoms, imaging) and history of immunotherapy as consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis.\nGR8: Matches with the three predicted statements that describe the lack of response to steroid treatment, increased oxygen requirements, and persistent symptoms/unchanged radiographs.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [\n      \"The patient developed new bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT after receiving ipilimumab and nivolumab, and had symptoms of fever and dyspnea, which are consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis. The lack of response to antibiotics and the presence of lymphocytosis in the bronchoalveolar lavage fluid also support this diagnosis.\",\n      \"The patient developed new bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT after receiving ipilimumab and nivolumab, and had symptoms such as fever and dyspnea, which are consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis. The lack of response to antibiotics and the presence of lymphocytosis in the bronchoalveolar lavage fluid also support this diagnosis.\",\n      \"The patient's symptoms of fever, dyspnea, and bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT, combined with the lack of response to antibiotics and the presence of lymphocytic predominance in bronchoalveolar lavage fluid, are consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis. The patient's history of treatment with ipilimumab and nivolumab also supports this diagnosis.\"\n    ],\n    \"4\": [],\n    \"5\": [\n      \"The patient's clinical presentation, laboratory results, and imaging findings are consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis, which is a known side effect of immunotherapy with ipilimumab and nivolumab. The lack of response to steroid treatment and the results of the bronchoalveolar lavage fluid and transbronchial lung biopsy support this diagnosis.\",\n      \"The patient's lack of response to high-dose methylprednisolone and prednisolone, as evidenced by the persistence of symptoms and unchanged chest radiographs, suggests steroid-refractory disease. The bronchoalveolar lavage fluid findings and transbronchial lung biopsy results also support this diagnosis.\"\n    ],\n    \"6\": [\n      \"The patient developed new bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT after receiving ipilimumab and nivolumab, and had symptoms of fever and dyspnea, which are consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis. The lack of response to antibiotics and the presence of lymphocytosis in the bronchoalveolar lavage fluid also support this diagnosis.\",\n      \"The patient developed new bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT after receiving ipilimumab and nivolumab, and had symptoms such as fever and dyspnea, which are consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis. The lack of response to antibiotics and the presence of lymphocytosis in the bronchoalveolar lavage fluid also support this diagnosis.\",\n      \"The patient's symptoms of fever, dyspnea, and bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT, combined with the lack of response to antibiotics and the presence of lymphocytic predominance in bronchoalveolar lavage fluid, are consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis. The patient's history of treatment with ipilimumab and nivolumab also supports this diagnosis.\",\n      \"The patient's clinical presentation, laboratory results, and imaging findings are consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis, which is a known side effect of immunotherapy with ipilimumab and nivolumab. The lack of response to steroid treatment and the results of the bronchoalveolar lavage fluid and transbronchial lung biopsy support this diagnosis.\",\n      \"The patient's lack of response to high-dose methylprednisolone and prednisolone, as evidenced by the persistence of symptoms and unchanged chest radiographs, suggests steroid-refractory disease. The bronchoalveolar lavage fluid findings and transbronchial lung biopsy results also support this diagnosis.\"\n    ],\n    \"7\": [\n      \"The patient developed new bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT after receiving ipilimumab and nivolumab, and had symptoms of fever and dyspnea, which are consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis. The lack of response to antibiotics and the presence of lymphocytosis in the bronchoalveolar lavage fluid also support this diagnosis.\",\n      \"The patient developed new bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT after receiving ipilimumab and nivolumab, and had symptoms such as fever and dyspnea, which are consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis. The lack of response to antibiotics and the presence of lymphocytosis in the bronchoalveolar lavage fluid also support this diagnosis.\",\n      \"The patient's symptoms of fever, dyspnea, and bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT, combined with the lack of response to antibiotics and the presence of lymphocytic predominance in bronchoalveolar lavage fluid, are consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis. The patient's history of treatment with ipilimumab and nivolumab also supports this diagnosis.\",\n      \"The patient's clinical presentation, laboratory results, and imaging findings are consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis, which is a known side effect of immunotherapy with ipilimumab and ni