  "8c98e34ae194057eedb489c7b2f81bbe358050a191b20972d106d1f558361aa7": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Considered hepatitis given fever and arthralgias — “hepatitis B antigen serology ... came back negative.”\n2. Considered viral illness (including dengue) — “The absence of fever, thrombocytopenia, malaise, and weakness ruled out other differential diagnoses such as rheumatic fever and dengue…”\n3. Considered acute rheumatic fever — “The absence of fever, thrombocytopenia, malaise, and weakness ruled out other differential diagnoses such as rheumatic fever and dengue…”\n4. Considered subacute bacterial endocarditis — “absence of infective endocarditis symptoms (petechiae, Osler node, Janeway node, splinter hemorrhage, sign of sepsis), sterile blood culture, normal cardiac examination (including inspection, palpation, percussion and auscultation).”\n5. Considered rheumatologic diseases — “hepatitis B antigen serology, antinuclear antibody, ELISA, direct Coombs, rheumatic factor, anti‐CCP, and LDH tests were done to rule out other conditions and came back negative.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\nDiagnosis: Prosthetic Valve Thrombosis\nEvidence: The patient has a history of prosthetic mitral valve and recent streptokinase treatment for suspected prosthetic mitral valve thrombosis, and now presents with symptoms of heart failure, such as dyspnea on exertion, orthopnea, and paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea, which could indicate a new or recurrent thrombosis.\nDiagnosis: Infective Endocarditis\nEvidence: The patient has a fever and elevated inflammatory markers (elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein), which could indicate an infectious process, and the presence of a prosthetic valve increases the risk of infective endocarditis.\n\nAnalysis of Groundtruth Diagnostic Reasons vs. Predicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n\nGroundtruth 1: Considered hepatitis given fever and arthralgias — “hepatitis B antigen serology ... came back negative.”\n- Potential match from Predicted Diagnostic Rea