natal ultrasound findings of bilateral renal enlargement and hydronephrosis, a markedly dilated fetal bladder, and the postnatal MRI and magnetic resonance urography results showing bladder wall irregularity and thickening, and bilateral ureteral dilation, could also be consistent with Prune Belly Syndrome. However, the presence of a left perirenal fluid collection and the absence of other characteristic features such as abdominal muscle weakness and undescended testes make PUV a more likely diagnosis.\",\n      \"While all panel members consider Prune Belly Syndrome as a possible diagnosis due to the prenatal ultrasound findings of bilateral renal enlargement, hydronephrosis, and a dilated fetal bladder, as well as the postnatal MRI and magnetic resonance urography results, they note that the absence of characteristic abdominal muscle deficiency and undescended testes makes PUV a more likely diagnosis.\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```",
