  "fdc3ba93af796cd2b5b37a8cb20c78ad03e99c53bc39a337ae76c4d813b794f8": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Ovarian cancer was suspected because of adnexal thickening and elevated CA-125 — “The radiologist suspected primary malignancy originating from thickening of bilateral adnexa which could be in the form of ovarian cancer.”\n2. A gynecologic origin was less likely after ultrasound showed no ovarian abnormalities — “There were no signs of abnormalities such as cancer or cysts on gynecological organs based on the USG.”\n3. Bone metastases were contemplated due to lytic, hypermetabolic bone lesions — “multiple osteolytic hypermetabolic metastases located in bone marrow of right humerus head, sternal bone region, several bilateral ribs, pelvic bone, and most of cervical/sacral vertebra bone.”\n4. Peritoneal carcinomatosis was considered from omental changes on PET — “presence of non-metabolic bleakness at the area of upper omentum with suspicion of metastatic seeding in the form of carcinomatosis omental.”\n5. Malignancy was questioned when fluid cytology showed no malignant cells — “Cytologic examination of pleural fluid showed lymphocytes, leukocytes, and mesothelial cells with no presence of malignant cells.”\n6. Tuberculous lymphadenitis was identified on lymph-node biopsy — “the biopsy showed tubercles surrounded by lymphocytes... the pathological anatomy report concluded the diagnosis as tuberculous lymphadenitis.”\n7. Abdominal tuberculosis was confirmed by laparoscopy and biopsy showing granulomas — “Omentum biopsy revealed granuloma containing epithelioid cells and Langhans-type cells. These results suggested tuberculosis infection of omentum and liver.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. The patient's symptoms of worsening dyspnea, dry cough, fatigue, and significant weight loss, along with laboratory findings of elevated CA-125 and imaging results showing bilateral pleural and pericardial effusions, lytic lesions in bones, and FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa on PET-CT, are highly suggestive of ovarian cancer, particularly given the presence of hypermetabolic osteolytic lesions and lymph nodes.\n2. The patient's presentation with dyspnea, dry cough, fatigue, weight loss, and abnormal labs (elevated CA-125, anemia) along with imaging findings (bilateral pleural effusions, pericardial effusion, lytic bone lesions, and FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa on PET-CT) suggests ovarian cancer with metastasis. The normal-sized ovaries on ultrasound do not rule out this diagnosis as ovarian cancer can present with metastatic disease even when the primary tumor is small.\n3. The patient's presentation with worsening dyspnea, dry cough, fatigue, and significant weight loss, along with laboratory findings of elevated CA-125 and imaging findings of bilateral pleural and pericardial effusions, mediastinal lymphadenopathy, and lytic bone lesions, are highly suggestive of ovarian cancer, particularly given the PET-CT findings of FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa.\n4. The patient has elevated CA-125 levels, bilateral adnexal thickening on PET-CT, and hypermetabolic lesions in multiple areas, which are suspicious for metastatic ovarian cancer. The lack of visible masses on ultrasound does not rule out this diagnosis, as some ovarian cancers can be difficult to detect by imaging.\n5. The presence of lytic lesions in the sternum, right humerus, and thoracic vertebrae, along with hypermetabolic lymph nodes, pericardium, and pleura on PET-CT, indicates widespread metastatic disease, which is consistent with advanced ovarian cancer.\n6. The presence of hypermetabolic osteolytic lesions in bone, hypermetabolic lymph nodes, pericardium, and pleura on PET-CT, along with the patient's clinical presentation and laboratory findings, support the diagnosis of metastatic disease, which is likely related to the primary ovarian malignancy.\n\nAnalysis of potential matches:\n\nGroundtruth 1: Ovarian cancer was suspected because of adnexal thickening and elevated CA-125 — “The radiologist suspected primary malignancy originating from thickening of bilateral adnexa which could be in the form of ovarian cancer.”\n*   Predicted 1: \"The patient's symptoms of worsening dyspnea, dry cough, fatigue, and significant weight loss, along with laboratory findings of elevated CA-125 and imaging results showing bilateral pleural and pericardial effusions, lytic lesions in bones, and FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa on PET-CT, are highly suggestive of ovarian cancer, particularly given the presence of hypermetabolic osteolytic lesions and lymph nodes.\"\n    *   Pros: Mentions \"elevated CA-125\" and \"FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa on PET-CT\" as suggestive of ovarian cancer.\n    *   Cons: Includes other symptoms and findings not explicitly in the ground truth statement.\n*   Predicted 2: \"The patient's presentation with dyspnea, dry cough, fatigue, weight loss, and abnormal labs (elevated CA-125, anemia) along with imaging findings (bilateral pleural effusions, pericardial effusion, lytic bone lesions, and FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa on PET-CT) suggests ovarian cancer with metastasis. The normal-sized ovaries on ultrasound do not rule out this diagnosis as ovarian cancer can present with metastatic disease even when the primary tumor is small.\"\n    *   Pros: Mentions \"elevated CA-125\" and \"FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa on PET-CT\" as suggesting ovarian cancer.\n    *   Cons: Includes other symptoms and findings not explicitly in the ground truth statement.\n*   Predicted 3: \"The patient's presentation with worsening dyspnea, dry cough, fatigue, and significant weight loss, along with laboratory findings of elevated CA-125 and imaging findings of bilateral pleural and pericardial effusions, mediastinal lymphadenopathy, and lytic bone lesions, are highly suggestive of ovarian cancer, particularly given the PET-CT findings of FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa.\"\n    *   Pros: Mentions \"elevated CA-125\" and \"PET-CT findings of FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa\" as highly suggestive of ovarian cancer.\n    *   Cons: Includes other symptoms and findings not explicitly in the ground truth statement.\n*   Predicted 4: \"The patient has elevated CA-125 levels, bilateral adnexal thickening on PET-CT, and hypermetabolic lesions in multiple areas, which are suspicious for metastatic ovarian cancer. The lack of visible masses on ultrasound does not rule out this diagnosis, as some ovarian cancers can be difficult to detect by imaging.\"\n    *   Pros: Directly states \"elevated CA-125 levels\" and \"bilateral adnexal thickening on PET-CT\" are suspicious for ovarian cancer. This is a very strong match.\n    *   Cons: None.\n\nGroundtruth 2: A gynecologic origin was less likely after ultrasound showed no ovarian abnormalities — “There were no signs of abnormalities such as cancer or cysts on gynecological organs based on the USG.”\n*   Predicted 2: \"The patient's presentation with dyspnea, dry cough, fatigue, weight loss, and abnormal labs (elevated CA-125, anemia) along with imaging findings (bilateral pleural effusions, pericardial effusion, lytic bone lesions, and FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa on PET-CT) suggests ovarian cancer with metastasis. The normal-sized ovaries on ultrasound do not rule out this diagnosis as ovarian cancer can present with metastatic disease even when the primary tumor is small.\"\n    *   Pros: Mentions \"normal-sized ovaries on ultrasound do not rule out this diagnosis\". This directly addresses the finding of no ovarian abnormalities on USG and its implication for the diagnosis.\n    *   Cons: The ground truth states it made a gynecologic origin *less likely*, while the predicted states it *does not rule out* the diagnosis. The nuance is slightly different, but the core idea of the ultrasound finding not definitively excluding ovarian cancer is present.\n*   Predicted 4: \"The patient has elevated CA-125 levels, bilateral adnexal thickening on PET-CT, and hypermetabolic lesions in multiple areas, which are suspicious for metastatic ovarian cancer. The lack of visible masses on ultrasound does not rule out this diagnosis, as some ovarian cancers can be difficult to detect by imaging.\"\n    *   Pros: States \"The lack of visible masses on ultrasound does not rule out this diagnosis\". This directly matches the sentiment of the ground truth.\n    *   Cons: None.\n\nGroundtruth 3: Bone metastases were contemplated due to lytic, hypermetabolic bone lesions — “multiple osteolytic hypermetabolic metastases located in bone marrow of right humerus head, sternal bone region, several bilateral ribs, pelvic bone, and most of cervical/sacral vertebra bone.”\n*   Predicted 1: \"...lactic lesions in bones... particularly given the presence of hypermetabolic osteolytic lesions...\"\n    *   Pros: Mentions \"lytic lesions in bones\" and \"hypermetabolic osteolytic lesions\".\n    *   Cons: Does not explicitly state \"bone metastases were contemplated due to\" but rather lists them as part of the overall picture. However, the presence of these lesions strongly implies contemplation of bone metastases.\n*   Predicted 2: \"...lytic bone lesions...\"\n    *   Pros: Mentions \"lytic bone lesions\".\n    *   Cons: Less detailed than Predicted 1, 3, 5, 6.\n*   Predicted 3: \"...lytic bone lesions...\"\n    *   Pros: Mentions \"lytic bone lesions\".\n    *   Cons: Less detailed than Predicted 1, 3, 5, 6.\n*   Predicted 5: \"The presence of lytic lesions in the sternum, right humerus, and thoracic vertebrae, along with hypermetabolic lymph nodes, pericardium, and pleura on PET-CT, indicates widespread metastatic disease, which is consistent with advanced ovarian cancer.\"\n    *   Pros: Explicitly mentions \"lytic lesions in the sternum, right humerus, and thoracic vertebrae\" and links them to \"widespread metastatic disease\". This is a very strong match.\n    *   Cons: None.\n*   Predicted 6: \"The presence of hypermetabolic osteolytic lesions in bone, hypermetabolic lymph nodes, pericardium, and pleura on PET-CT, along with the patient's clinical presentation and laboratory findings, support the diagnosis of metastatic disease, which is likely related to the primary ovarian malignancy.\"\n    *   Pros: Explicitly mentions \"hypermetabolic osteolytic lesions in bone\" and links them to \"metastatic disease\". This is a very strong match.\n    *   Cons: None.\n\nGroundtruth 4: Peritoneal carcinomatosis was considered from omental changes on PET — “presence of non-metabolic bleakness at the area of upper omentum with suspicion of metastatic seeding in the form of carcinomatosis omental.”\n*   None of the predicted statements mention \"peritoneal carcinomatosis\" or \"omental changes\" or \"non-metabolic bleakness at the area of upper omentum\".\n\nGroundtruth 5: Malignancy was questioned when fluid cytology showed no malignant cells — “Cytologic examination of pleural fluid showed lymphocytes, leukocytes, and mesothelial cells with no presence of malignant cells.”\n*   None of the predicted statements mention \"fluid cytology\" or \"no malignant cells\".\n\nGroundtruth 6: Tuberculous lymphadenitis was identified on lymph-node biopsy — “the biopsy showed tubercles surrounded by lymphocytes... the pathological anatomy report concluded the diagnosis as tuberculous lymphadenitis.”\n*   None of the predicted statements mention \"tuberculous lymphadenitis\" or \"tubercles\" or \"lymph-node biopsy\" leading to this diagnosis.\n\nGroundtruth 7: Abdominal tuberculosis was confirmed by laparoscopy and biopsy showing granulomas — “Omentum biopsy revealed granuloma containing epithelioid cells and Langhans-type cells. These results suggested tuberculosis infection of omentum and liver.”\n*   None of the predicted statements mention \"abdominal tuberculosis\" or \"laparoscopy\" or \"granulomas\" or \"omentum biopsy\" leading to this diagnosis.\n\nSummary of final matching decisions:\n*   Groundtruth 1 matches Predicted 1, 2, 3, and 4 because they all mention elevated CA-125 and adnexal thickening as suggestive of ovarian cancer. Predicted 4 is the most direct match.\n*   Groundtruth 2 matches Predicted 2 and 4 because they both state that the lack of ovarian abnormalities on ultrasound does not rule out ovarian cancer.\n*   Groundtruth 3 matches Predicted 1, 5, and 6 because they all mention lytic/hypermetabolic bone lesions as indicative of metastatic disease. Predicted 5 and 6 are the most direct and detailed matches.\n*   Groundtruth 4 has no matches.\n*   Groundtruth 5 has no matches.\n*   Groundtruth 6 has no matches.\n*   Groundtruth 7 has no matches.\n\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [\n      \"The patient's symptoms of worsening dyspnea, dry cough, fatigue, and significant weight loss, along with laboratory findings of elevated CA-125 and imaging results showing bilateral pleural and pericardial effusions, lytic lesions in bones, and FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa on PET-CT, are highly suggestive of ovarian cancer, particularly given the presence of hypermetabolic osteolytic lesions and lymph nodes.\",\n      \"The patient's presentation with dyspnea, dry cough, fatigue, weight loss, and abnormal labs (elevated CA-125, anemia) along with imaging findings (bilateral pleural effusions, pericardial effusion, lytic bone lesions, and FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa on PET-CT) suggests ovarian cancer with metastasis. The normal-sized ovaries on ultrasound do not rule out this diagnosis as ovarian cancer can present with metastatic disease even when the primary tumor is small.\",\n      \"The patient's presentation with worsening dyspnea, dry cough, fatigue, and significant weight loss, along with laboratory findings of elevated CA-125 and imaging findings of bilateral pleural and pericardial effusions, mediastinal lymphadenopathy, and lytic bone lesions, are highly suggestive of ovarian cancer, particularly given the PET-CT findings of FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa.\",\n      \"The patient has elevated CA-125 levels, bilateral adnexal thickening on PET-CT, and hypermetabolic lesions in multiple areas, which are suspicious for metastatic ovarian cancer. The lack of visible masses on ultrasound does not rule out this diagnosis, as some ovarian cancers can be difficult to detect by imaging.\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [\n      \"The patient's presentation with dyspnea, dry cough, fatigue, weight loss, and abnormal labs (elevated CA-125, anemia) along with imaging findings (bilateral pleural effusions, pericardial effusion, lytic bone lesions, and FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa on PET-CT) suggests ovarian cancer with metastasis. The normal-sized ovaries on ultrasound do not rule out this diagnosis as ovarian cancer can present with metastatic disease even when the primary tumor is small.\",\n      \"The patient has elevated CA-125 levels, bilateral adnexal thickening on PET-CT, and hypermetabolic lesions in multiple areas, which are suspicious for metastatic ovarian cancer. The lack of visible masses on ultrasound does not rule out this diagnosis, as some ovarian cancers can be difficult to detect by imaging.\"\n    ],\n    \"3\": [\n      \"The patient's symptoms of worsening dyspnea, dry cough, fatigue, and significant weight loss, along with laboratory findings of elevated CA-125 and imaging results showing bilateral pleural and pericardial effusions, lytic lesions in bones, and FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa on PET-CT, are highly suggestive of ovarian cancer, particularly given the presence of hypermetabolic osteolytic lesions and lymph nodes.\",\n      \"The presence of lytic lesions in the sternum, right humerus, and thoracic vertebrae, along with hypermetabolic lymph nodes, pericardium, and pleura on PET-CT, indicates widespread metastatic disease, which is consistent with advanced ovarian cancer.\",\n      \"The presence of hypermetabolic osteolytic lesions in bone, hypermetabolic lymph nodes, pericardium, and pleura on PET-CT, along with the patient's clinical presentation and laboratory findings, support the diagnosis of metastatic disease, which is likely related to the primary ovarian malignancy.\"\n    ],\n    \"4\": [],\n    \"5\": [],\n    \"6\": [],\n    \"7\": []\n  }\n}\n```",
