 response to antibiotics\" also supports ruling out bacterial infection.\n    - Cons: Not explicitly mentioning Gram stain.\n- Predicted Diagnostic Reason 2: \"the lack of response to antibiotics and corticosteroids, and the negative results for other infections.\"\n    - Pros: \"Lack of response to antibiotics\" and \"negative results for other infections\" are relevant.\n    - Cons: Not explicitly mentioning Gram stain.\n- Predicted Diagnostic Reason 3: \"The lack of response to antibiotics and corticosteroids, along with the negative tests for other infections, increases the likelihood of Kaposi's sarcoma.\"\n    - Pros: \"Lack of response to antibiotics\" and \"negative tests for other infections\" are relevant.\n    - Cons: Not explicitly mentioning Gram stain.\n- Predicted Diagnostic Reason 4: \"The lack of response to antibiotics and corticosteroids, along with negative tests for other infections, further supports this diagnosis.\"\n    - Pros: \"Lack of response to antibiotics\" and \"negative tests for other infections\" are relevant.\n    - Cons: Not explicitly mentioning Gram stain.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 4: Fungal infection (broad) — “fungal blood cultures, and urine and serum Histoplasma antigen were negative.”\n- Predicted Diagnostic Reason 1: \"The lack of response to antibiotics and the negative results of various infectious disease tests increase the likelihood of Kaposi's sarcoma.\"\n    - Pros: \"Negative results of various infectious disease tests\" could encompass fungal cultures and Histoplasma antigen tests.\n    - Cons: Not explicitly mentioning fungal tests.\n- Predicted Diagnostic Reason 2: \"the negative results for other infections.\"\n    - Pros: General statement about negative infection tests.\n    - Cons: Not specific to fungal tests.\n- Predicted Diagnostic Reason 3: \"the negative tests for other infections.\"\n    - Pros: General statement about negative infection tests.\n    - Cons: Not specific to fungal tests.\n- Predicted Diagnostic Reason 4: \"negative tests for other infections.\"\n    - Pros: General statement about negative infection tests.\n    - Cons: Not specific to fungal tests.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 5: Other endemic mycoses (Aspergillus, Blastomyces, Coccidioides) — “Serum immunodiffusion assay was strongly positive for H. capsulatum ‘H’ and ‘M’ bands but negative for Aspergillus fumigatus, Blastomyces dermatiditis, and Coccidiodes immitis.”\n- Predicted Diagnostic Reason 1: \"The lack of response to antibiotics and the negative results of various infectious disease tests increase the likelihood of Kaposi's sarcoma.\"\n    - Pros: \"Negative results of various infectious disease tests\" could encompass these specific fungal tests.\n    - Cons: Not explicitly mentioning these specific mycoses.\n- Predicted Diagnostic Reason 2: \"the negative results for other infections.\"\n    - Pros: General statement about negative infection tests.\n    - Cons: Not specific to these mycoses.\n- Predicted Diagnostic Reason 3: \"the negative tests for other infections.\"\n    - Pros: General statement about negative infection tests.\n    - Cons: Not specific to these mycoses.\n- Predicted Diagnostic Reason 4: \"negative tests for other infections.\"\n    - Pros: General statement about negative infection tests.\n    - Cons: Not specific to these mycoses.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 6: Histoplasma capsulatum infection — “Complement fixation test was positive for H. capsulatum mycelial and yeast forms (titer > 1:256).”\n- There are no predicted reasons that mention a positive test for Histoplasma capsulatum. All predicted reasons focus on ruling out infections or supporting Kaposi's sarcoma.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 7: Histopathologic confirmation — “Biopsy of the lips revealed … presence of small budding yeast forms.”\n- There are no predicted reasons that mention biopsy findings or the presence of yeast forms. All predicted reasons focus on ruling out infections 