 Mentions \"ultrasound... compatible with appendicitis.\"\n    - Cons: Does not specifically mention \"plastron appendicitis.\" The groundtruth is very specific about \"plastron appendicitis.\"\n- Predicted 2: \"The patient's symptoms of abdominal pain that began around the umbilicus and migrated to the right lower quadrant, along with the ultrasound assessment compatible with plastron appendicitis and CT findings of a linear lesion within the intestinal lumen in the right lower quadrant, are highly suggestive of appendicitis. The elevated white-cell count also supports an infectious or inflammatory process, which is consistent with appendicitis.\"\n    - Pros: Directly states \"ultrasound assessment compatible with plastron appendicitis,\" which is a perfect match.\n    - Cons: None for this specific match.\n- Predicted 3: \"The patient's symptoms, including abdominal pain that migrated to the right lower quadrant, and laboratory tests, such as an elevated white-cell count, are consistent with appendicitis. Imaging findings, including the ultrasound assessment and computed tomography of the abdomen, also support this diagnosis, showing a linear lesion within the intestinal lumen in the right lower quadrant and edema on the anterolateral aspect of the cecum.\"\n    - Pros: Mentions \"ultrasound assessment\" as supporting the diagnosis.\n    - Cons: Does not specifically mention \"plastron appendicitis.\"\n\nGroundtruth 3: Peptic ulcer perforation was less likely given the absence of free intraperitoneal air on X-ray — “there was no free air under the diaphragm at X-rays.”\n- Predicted: No predicted statements mention the absence of free intraperitoneal air or rule out peptic ulcer perforation.\n\nGroundtruth 4: Foreign-body perforation was raised when CT demonstrated an intraluminal linear lesion causing partial obstruction and adjacent edema — “Computerized tomography (CT) scans revealed a linear lesion in the intestinal lumen of the right lower quadrant, causing a partial luminal obliteration and edema…”\n- Predicted 1: \"The patient's symptoms of abdominal pain that migrated to the right lower quadrant, voluntary guarding, and minimal tenderness elsewhere, along with laboratory tests showing a high white-cell count and imaging results (plain abdominal X-rays, ultrasound, and computed tomography) compatible with appendicitis, support this diagnosis.\"\n    - Pros: Mentions \"computed tomography) compatible with appendicitis.\"\n    - Cons: Does not specify the \"linear lesion,\" \"partial obstruction,\" or \"adjacent edema\" as the groundtruth does.\n- Predicted 2: \"The patient's symptoms of abdominal pain that began around the umbilicus and migrated to the right lower quadrant, along with the ultrasound assessment compatible with plastron appendicitis and CT findings of a linear lesion within the intestinal lumen in the right lower quadrant, are highly suggestive of appendicitis. The elevated white-cell count also supports an infectious or inflammatory process, which is consistent with appendicitis.\"\n    - Pros: Mentions \"CT findings of a linear lesion within the intestinal lumen in the right lower quadrant,\" which is a strong match for the description of the lesion.\n    - Cons: Does not explicitly mention \"partial obstruction\" or \"adjacent edema\" in this specific statement, though it implies the significance of the lesion.\n- Predicted 3: \"The patient's symptoms, including abdominal pain that migrated to the right lower quadrant, and laboratory tests, such as an elevated white-cell count, are consistent with appendicitis. Imaging findings, including the ultrasound assessment and computed tomography of the abdomen, also support this diagnosis, showing a linear lesion within the intestinal lumen in the right lower quadrant and edema on the anterolateral aspect of the cecum.\"\n    - Pros: Mentions \"computed tomography of the abdomen, also support this diagnosis, showing a linear lesion within the intestinal lumen in the right lower quadrant and edema on the anterolateral aspect of the cecum.\" This is a very close match, including the linear lesion and edema.\n    - Cons: Does not explicitly mention \"partial obstruction.\"\n- Predicted 4: \"The presence of several air–fluid levels on plain abdominal X-rays and the computed tomography findings of a linear lesion within the intestinal lumen causing partial luminal obliteration and edema suggest a possible intestinal obstruction, which could be related to or complicated by the appendicitis.\"\n    - Pros: Directly mentions \"computed tomography findings of a linear lesion within the intestinal lumen causing partial luminal obliteration and edema,\" which is an almost perfect match to the groundtruth description of the CT findings. It also links it to \"intestinal obstruction,\" which is implied by \"partial obstruction\" in the groundtruth.\n    - Cons: None for this specific match.\n\nGroundtruth 5: Other rare causes (urinary tract pathology, inflammatory bowel disease, intestinal parasitosis, malignancy, Meckel’s diverticulitis) were recognized as possibilities in the differential but were not supported by clinical or imaging findings — “Urinary tract pathologies, inflammatory bowel diseases, peptic ulcer perforations, intestinal parasitosis, malignancies, and Meckel’s diverticulitis should be considered in differential diagnosis of such a patient.”\n- Predicted: No predicted statements discuss other differential diagnoses or rule them out.\n\nSummary of final matching decisions:\n- Groundtruth 1 matches Predicted 1, Predicted 2, and Predicted 3 because all three mention migratory right lower quadrant pain and elevated white blood cell count in the context of appendicitis.\n- Groundtruth 2 matches Predicted 2 because it explicitly mentions \"ultrasound assessment compatible with plastron appendicitis.\"\n- Groundtruth 3 has no direct match in the predicted reasons.\n- Groundtruth 4 matches Predicted 2, Predicted 3, and Predicted 4. Predicted 4 is the strongest match as it includes \"linear lesion,\" \"partial luminal obliteration,\" and \"edema.\" Predicted 2 and 3 also mention the \"linear lesion\" and \"edema\" respectively.\n- Groundtruth 5 has no direct match in the predicted reasons.\n\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [\n      \"The patient's symptoms of abdominal pain that migrated to the right lower quadrant, voluntary guarding, and minimal tenderness elsewhere, along with laboratory tests showing a high white-cell count and imaging results (plain abdominal X-rays, ultrasound, and computed tomography) compatible with appendicitis, support this diagnosis.\",\n      \"The patient's symptoms of abdominal pain that began around the umbilicus and migrated to the right lower quadrant, along with the ultrasound assessment compatible with plastron appendicitis and CT findings of a linear lesion within the intestinal lumen in the right lower quadrant, are highly suggestive of appendicitis. The elevated white-cell count also supports an infectious or inflammatory process, which is consistent with appendicitis.\",\n      \"The patient's symptoms, including abdominal pain that migrated to the right lower quadrant, and laboratory tests, such as an elevated white-cell count, are consistent with appendicitis. Imaging findings, including the ultrasound assessment and computed tomography of the abdomen, also support this diagnosis, showing a linear lesion within the intestinal lumen in the right lower quadrant and edema on the anterolateral aspect of the cecum.\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [\n      \"The patient's symptoms of abdominal pain that began around the umbilicus and migrated to the right lower quadrant, along with the ultrasound assessment compatible with plastron appendicitis and CT findings of a linear lesion within the intestinal lumen in the right lower quadrant, are highly suggestive of appendicitis. The elevated white-cell count also supports an