    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Wernicke’s encephalopathy was considered but excluded because “The main MRI findings in Wernicke’s encephalopathy were reported to be in the area around the third ventricle and the middle cerebral aqueduct…”\n2. Hashimoto’s encephalopathy was considered but excluded because “in Hashimoto’s encephalopathy, cerebral white matter lesions and limbic system lesions were reported to be the main findings…”\n3. Cerebellar infarction was excluded because “MRI images also improved; therefore, it was not a cerebellar infarction…”\n4. Heat stroke–induced cerebellar injury was favored given that “Heat stroke damages Purkinje cells in the cerebellum, which have low tolerance to heat.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons (three statements):\n- Statement A: \"The patient's presentation with a high body temperature of 42.4 °C, altered mental status, and systemic complications such as hepatic dysfunction, renal dysfunction, and disseminated intravascular coagulation during a heat wave is consistent with heat stroke. The initial APACHE II score of 42 and SOFA score of 15 also indicate severe illness.\"\n- Statement B: \"The patient's presentation with a high body temperature of 42.4 °C, altered mental status, and systemic involvement such as hepatic dysfunction, renal dysfunction, and disseminated intravascular coagulation during a heat wave is consistent with heat stroke. The APACHE II score of 42 and SOFA score of 15 also indicate severe disease.\"\n- Statement C: \"The subsequent brain MRI showing bilateral cerebellar hemisphere high-signal intensity on diffusion-weighted and FLAIR images with corresponding low ADC signals, and the SPECT with 123I-IMP demonstrating decreased perfusion in the right cerebellar hemisphere, suggest cerebellar infarction as a complication of the heat stroke, possibly due to hypoperfusion or coagulopathy.\"\n\nNow evaluate each GT statement:\n\nGT1: Wernicke's encephalopathy exclusion based on MRI findings. No statement in PD mentions Wernicke's encephalopathy or its MRI findings. Pros: none. Cons: no commonality. No match.\n\nGT2: Hashimoto's encephalopathy exclusion based on white matter/limbic lesions. No statement in PD mentions Hashimoto's or those findings. No match.\n\nGT3: Cerebellar infarction excluded because MRI improved. Statement C actually suggests cerebellar infarction is present (not excluded) based on MRI findings. This is opposite reasoning. Pros: mentions cerebellar MRI findings. Cons: conclusion is contrary (present vs excluded). Not equivalent. No match.\n\nGT4: Heat stroke-induced cerebellar injury favored because heat damages Purkinje cells. Statements A and B support heat stroke diagnosis generally, but do not specifically mention cerebellar injury or Purkinje cells. Statement C mentions cerebellar infarction as a complication, not direct heat injury. The reasoning about Purkinje cell sensitivity is absent. Pros: heat stroke is favored. Cons: the specific justification (Purkinje cell damage) is not present. No match.\n\nThus, no matching statements for any GT statement.\n\nFinal matching decisions: all empty arrays.\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": []\n  }\n}\n```",
