  "77e530a5643804918230f1c4ce5d516ca74a55a52228b45d82d3ba6a42afe1ec": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Lymphoma relapse — “computed tomographic scan showed no signs of a lymphoma relapse.”\n2. Autoimmune myositis — “Additional myositis panel testing did not indicate an autoimmune reaction.”\n3. Therapy-related side effects — “Administration of systemic steroids and transient discontinuation of ibrutinib had no effect.”\n4. Neurological disease — “Further neurological diagnostics only confirmed mild neuropathy.”\n5. Infectious disease such as lues or borreliosis — “Differential diagnoses included … infectious disease such as lues or borreliosis.”\n6. Viral myositis — “Biopsy revealed signs of viral myositis.”\n7. Enterovirus infection — “extended microbiological and virological screening revealed a positive enterovirus polymerase chain reaction (PCR) in the CSF” and “enterovirus RNA was also found in the patient’s serum, stool samples, and muscle biopsy.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons (each evidence statement listed separately):\nP1: \"The patient's symptoms of muscle pain, swelling, and elevated CK levels, along with the muscle biopsy findings, are consistent with an inflammatory myopathy. The temporal association with ibrutinib use and the lack of response to discontinuation of the medication suggest a possible association with the drug. The presence of interstitial and perivascular T-cell and macrophage infiltrates on muscle biopsy also supports this diagnosis.\"\nP2: \"The patient's symptoms of muscle pain, swelling, and elevated CK levels, along with the muscle biopsy findings, are consistent with an inflammatory myopathy. The temporal association with ibrutinib use and the lack of response to discontinuation of the medication suggest a possible association with the drug.\"\nP3: \"The patient presents with muscle pain, elevated CK, and muscle biopsy demonstrating interstitial and perivascular T-cell and macrophage infiltrates, which are consistent with myositis. The patient's symptoms and laboratory results, including elevated CRP, transaminases, and myoglobin, also support this diagnosis.\"\nP4: \"The patient presents with muscle pain, elevated CK, and muscle biopsy demonstrating interstitial and perivascular T-cell and macrophage infiltrates, which are consistent with myositis.\"\n\nAnalysis of matches for each groundtruth statement:\n\nStatement 1 (Lymphoma relapse): No predicted statement mentions CT scan or lymphoma. No match.\n\nStatement 2 (Autoimmune myositis): No predicted statement mentions myositis panel or autoimmune testing. No match.\n\nStatement 3 (Therapy-related side effects): Predicted P1 and P2 both state \"lack of response to discontinuation of the medication\". This directly matches the groundtruth's mention that discontinuation of ibrutinib had no effect. The groundtruth also includes steroids, but the core justification (lack of response to stopping the drug) is equivalent. Pros: Both refer to the same clinical observation. Cons: Groundtruth includes steroids, but predicted does not; however, the key point is the lack of effect of discontinuation, which is present. Therefore, P1 and P2 are matches.\n\nStatement 4 (Neurological disease): No predicted statement mentions neurological diagnostics or neuropathy. No match.\n\nStatement 5 (Infectious disease such as lues or borreliosis): No predicted statement mentions these infections or differential diagnoses. No match.\n\nStatement 6 (Viral myositis): Predicted statements mention muscle biopsy findings consistent with myositis, but do not specify viral etiology. Groundtruth explicitly states \"signs of viral myositis\". The predicted statements are broader and do not indicate viral cause. Not equivalent. No match.\n\nStatement 7 (Enterovirus infection): No predicted statement mentions enterovirus, PCR, or virological screening. No match.\n\nSummary of matching decisions:\n- Only groundtruth statement 3 has matches: P1 and P2.\n- All other groundtruth statements have no matches.\n\nJSON output:\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [\n      \"The patient's symptoms of muscle pain, swelling, and elevated CK levels, along with the muscle biopsy findings, are consistent with an inflammatory myopathy. The temporal association with ibrutinib use and the lack of response to discontinuation of the medication suggest a possible association with the drug. The presence of interstitial and perivascular T-cell and macrophage infiltrates on muscle biopsy also supports this diagnosis.\",\n      \"The patient's symptoms of muscle pain, swelling, and elevated CK levels, along with the muscle biopsy findings, are consistent with an inflammatory myopathy. The temporal association with ibrutinib use and the lack of response to discontinuation of the medication suggest a possible association with the drug.\"\n    ],\n    \"4\": [],\n    \"5\": [],\n    \"6\": [],\n    \"7\": []\n  }\n}",
    "prompt_id": "mcr.reasoning_recall"
  },
