  "7bc8174e307b1bcd95b634483149bbdd322e4994b07f428f0f040106d8f17a53": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. A Bartholin gland cyst was initially considered but excluded because of the patient’s age and absence of pain or discomfort with activity — “The cyst was excluded, because it is more common in young women, and causes symptoms of pain during walking, sitting, and intercourse, which were absent in this case.”\n2. Chondroid syringoma was diagnosed three times due to the lesion’s slow growth, encapsulation, and painless, solid features — “CS — which is also known as pleomorphic adenoma or benign mixed tumor — is a rare encapsulated, slow-growing, well-defined, painless and solid tumor.”\n3. Clear cell hidradenoma was considered after the second excision because of the lobular architecture of cuboidal cells without atypia — “lobular glandular proliferation of cuboid cells with no anaplasia.”\n4. Adenoid cystic carcinoma became the leading diagnosis when histology showed a characteristic cribriform architecture with nests of basaloid cells amid hyalinized stroma — “The cribriform pattern is the most typical characterization, which is formed by nests of tumor cells interrupted by basophilic basement membrane materials.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons (verbatim):\nP1: \"The patient has a history of recurrent masses in the labia majora, with previous histopathology showing chondroid syringoma. The most recent excised mass was also reported as chondroid syringoma, suggesting a strong likelihood of this diagnosis.\"\nP2: \"The patient has a history of recurrent masses in the labia majora, with previous histopathology showing chondroid syringoma. The most recent excision also reported chondroid syringoma, suggesting a strong likelihood of this diagnosis.\"\nP3: \"The patient has a history of recurrent masses in the labia majora, with previous histopathology showing chondroid syringoma. The most recent excised mass was also reported as chondroid syringoma, suggesting a benign but locally recurrent tumor.\"\nP4: \"The patient's history includes a previous diagnosis of clear cell hidradenoma, which is a rare tumor that can present similarly to chondroid syringoma. The recurrence of the mass near the fourchette could potentially be related to this diagnosis.\"\nP5: \"The patient's history includes a previous histology showing clear cell hidradenoma, and the current presentation of a recurrent mass near the fourchette could be consistent with this diagnosis, given the previous occurrence of this condition in the same area.\"\nP6: \"One of the patient's previous excised masses showed clear cell hidradenoma, which is a rare benign tumor. Although the most recent mass was reported as chondroid syringoma, the possibility of clear cell hidradenoma cannot be entirely ruled out, especially given the patient's history of both diagnoses.\"\n\nNow, for each groundtruth statement, consider potential matches:\n\n**Groundtruth 1 (Bartholin gland cyst exclusion):**\n- Potential matches: None of the predicted statements mention Bartholin gland cyst, age, pain, or exclusion criteria.\n- Pros: None.\n- Cons: No overlap in content.\n- Decision: No match.\n\n**Groundtruth 2 (Chondroid syringoma diagnosed three times due to slow growth, encapsulation, painless, solid features):**\n- Potential matches: P1, P2, P3 all mention chondroid syringoma based on histopathology history. However, they do not mention the specific clinical features (slow growth, encapsulation, painless, solid). The groundtruth justification is based on those features; the predicted justification is based on repeated histopathological diagnoses. These are different rationales.\n- Pros: Both refer to chondroid syringoma being diagnosed multiple times.\n- Cons: The specific features that led to the diagnosis in groundtruth are absent in predicted. The predicted statements focus on history of previous histology, not on the lesion's characteristics.\n- Decision: No match.\n\n**Groundtruth 3 (Clear cell hidradenoma considered after second excision due to lobular architecture of cuboidal cells without atypia):**\n- Potential matches: P4, P5, P6 mention clear cell hidradenoma based on previous diagnosis or history. They do not mention lobular architecture, cuboidal cells, or absence of atypia. The groundtruth reason is histological; predicted reasons are historical.\n- Pros: Both mention clear cell hidradenoma.\n- Cons: Different justifications; predicted statements do not reference the specific histological finding.\n- Decision: No match.\n\n**Groundtruth 4 (Adenoid cystic carcinoma leading diagnosis due to cribriform architecture):**\n- Potential matches: None of the predicted statements mention adenoid cystic carcinoma, cribriform pattern, basaloid cells, or hyalinized stroma.\n- Pros: None.\n- Cons: No overlap.\n- Decision: No match.\n\nSummary of matching decisions:\n- Groundtruth 1: No matches.\n- Groundtruth 2: No matches.\n- Groundtruth 3: No matches.\n- Groundtruth 4: No matches.\n\nThus, all groundtruth statements have empty matching lists.\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n\n<output>\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": []\n  }\n}\n</output>",
    "prompt_id": "mcr.reasoning_recall"
  },
  "8dac36fc2b70bb13d0af37eedd89cfd66e96703dcb8c5a7e140df846e954ff84": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\n1. Groundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n- G1: Hematoma of the spermatic cord — “he did not report any trauma… and the size of the lump did not change significantly during this two-month period.”\n- G2: Granuloma — “Usually, there is no correlation between the tumor and previous scrotal inflammation or trauma.”\n- G3: Sarcoma — “preoperative imaging study showed a well-demarcated mass, without evidence of unclear boundaries or invasion into the adjacent structures.”\n- G4: Seminoma — “In older patients, when intraparenchymal involvement is noticed, differential diagnosis should necessarily include seminoma.”\n- G5: Metastatic tumor — “the values of the specific tumor markers AFP, LDH, CEA, and b-HCG were normal.”\n- G6: Lipoma — “Differential diagnosis includes all possible testicular and paratesticular masses as well as other scrotal abnormalities such as lipoma, sarcoma, metastatic tumor, granuloma, and hematoma of the spermatic cord.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n- P1: “The patient presents with a painless, round, hazelnut-sized mass localized to the tail of the right epididymis, which is consistent with an epididymal cyst or spermatocele. The ultrasonography findings of a solid, well-demarcated, hyperechoic, hypoperfused mass with two small hypoechoic foci also support this diagnosis.”\n- P2: “Although less common, the ultrasonography findings could also be consistent with an epididymal tumor, such as an adenomatoid tumor. However, the lack of invasion of adjacent structures and normal serum tumor markers make this diagnosis less likely than an epididymal cyst or spermatocele.”\n- P3: “Although less common, the ultrasonography findings could also be consistent with an epididymal tumor, such as an adenomatoid tumor. The presence of a solid, well-demarcated mass with hypoechoic foci could suggest a neoplastic process, and the normal serum tumor markers do not entirely rule out this possibility.”\n\n2. Matching analysis:\n- G1: No predicted statement mentions trauma, absence of trauma, or stability of the lump over time. No match.\n- G2: No predicted statement mentions scrotal inflammation, trauma, or correlation with previous inflammation/trauma. No match.\n- G3: P1 mentions “well-demarcated” mass. P3 also mentions “well-demarcated mass.” P2 mentions “lack of invasion of adjacent structures.” Together these match the groundtruth imaging finding of a well-demarcated mass without invasion. Match with P1, P2, P3.\n- G4: No predicted statement mentions older patients, intraparenchymal involvement, or seminoma. No match.\n- G5: P2 and P3 both mention “normal serum tumor markers,” which matches the groundtruth statement about normal AFP, LDH, CEA, and b-HCG. Match with P2 and P3.\n- G6: No predicted statement lists lipoma, sarcoma, metastatic tumor, granuloma, hematoma, or a broad differential diagnosis. No match.\n\n3. Final matching decisions:\n- G1: []\n- G2: []\n- G3: [P1, P2, P3]\n- G4: []\n- G5: [P2, P3]\n- G6: []\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [\n      \"The patient presents with a painless, round, hazelnut-sized mass localized to the tail of the right epididymis, which is consistent with an epididymal cyst or spermatocele. The ultrasonography findings of a solid, well-demarcated, hyperechoic, hypoperfused mass with two small hypoechoic foci also support this diagnosis.\",\n      \"Although less common, the ultrasonography findings could also be consistent with an epididymal tumor, such as an adenomatoid tumor. However, the lack of invasion of adjacent structures and normal serum tumor markers make this diagnosis less likely than an epididymal cyst or spermatocele.\",\n      \"Although less common, the ultrasonography findings could also be consistent with an epididymal tumor, such as an adenomatoid tumor. The presence of a solid, well-demarcated mass with hypoechoic foci could suggest a neoplastic process, and the normal serum tumor markers do not entirely rule out this possibility.\"\n    ],\n    \"4\": [],\n    \"5\": [\n      \"Although less common, the ultrasonography findings could also be consistent with an epididymal tumor, such as an adenomatoid tumor. However, the lack of invasion of adjacent structures and normal serum tumor markers make this diagnosis less likely than an epididymal cyst or spermatocele.\",\n      \"Although less common, the ultrasonography findings could also be consistent with an epididymal tumor, such as an adenomatoid tumor. The presence of a solid, well-demarcated mass with hypoechoic foci could suggest a neoplastic process, and the normal serum tumor markers do not entirely rule out this possibility.\"\n    ],\n    \"6\": []\n  }\n}\n```",
