  "5a0dc91d0aa484ad568098ea0ae46d6b7d9e368f1aae29e9ab83e69c44908b0d": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons (GDR):\n1. Gastric outlet obstruction was considered given the patient’s intractable vomiting and abdominal distension — “…differential diagnoses were considered given the above presentation: • Gastric outlet obstruction.”\n2. Peptic stricture of the stomach was considered as a cause of outlet narrowing — “…differential diagnoses were considered given the above presentation: • Peptic stricture of the stomach.”\n3. Neoplasm of the stomach was considered because tumors can obstruct gastric outflow — “…differential diagnoses were considered given the above presentation: • Neoplasm of the stomach.”\n4. Annular pancreas, diabetic gastroparesis, internal hernia, and duodenal bezoar were considered as alternative causes of obstruction but were excluded by endoscopy — “An upper gastrointestinal endoscopy was also done to rule out other causes and it was found to be normal.”\n5. Peptic ulcer disease and duodenal tumors were also considered among other causes of obstruction and were excluded by imaging and endoscopy — “…other causes of duodenal obstruction like peptic ulcer disease and duodenal tumors.”\n6. Scleroderma and other collagen or vascular disorders were considered as mimics of the presentation — “…conditions that closely mimic SMA syndrome ranging from scleroderma to other collagen and vascular disorders…”\n7. Vascular compression due to a reduced aortomesenteric angle was supported by CT findings demonstrating duodenal compression — “CT scans were used to rule in for duodenal obstruction due to a reduced aortomesenteric angle in the patient.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons (PDR) – each block is a separate statement:\nPDR1: \"The patient's symptoms of intractable vomiting, postprandial upper abdominal pain, and bloating, combined with the findings of marked gastric distension and compression of the third portion of the duodenum between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta at an acute angle on CT, are consistent with Superior Mesenteric Artery Syndrome. The patient's lean body habitus and recent fasting may have contributed to the compression.\"\nPDR2: \"The patient's symptoms of intractable vomiting, postprandial upper abdominal pain, and bloating, combined with the findings of marked gastric distension and compression of the third portion of the duodenum between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta at an acute angle on CT, are consistent with Superior Mesenteric Artery Syndrome. The patient's lean body habitus and recent history of fasting may have contributed to the development of this condition.\"\nPDR3: \"The patient's symptoms of intractable vomiting, postprandial upper abdominal pain, and bloating, combined with the imaging findings of marked gastric distension and compression of the third portion of the duodenum between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta, are consistent with Superior Mesenteric Artery Syndrome. The patient's lean body habitus and recent fasting may have contributed to the narrowing of the angle between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta, leading to duodenal compression.\"\nPDR4: \"The patient's symptoms of vomiting, abdominal pain, and bloating, along with the finding of marked gastric distension, could also be consistent with a gastric outlet obstruction. However, the normal upper gastrointestinal endoscopy and the specific finding of compression of the duodenum between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta make Superior Mesenteric Artery Syndrome a more likely diagnosis.\"\nPDR5: \"The patient's symptoms of vomiting, abdominal pain, and bloating, along with the finding of marked gastric distension, could also be consistent with a gastric outlet obstruction. However, the normal upper gastrointestinal endoscopy and the specific finding of compression of the third portion of the duodenum between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta make Superior Mesenteric Artery Syndrome a more likely diagnosis.\"\nPDR6: \"The patient's symptoms of vomiting, abdominal pain, and bloating, along with the imaging findings of marked gastric distension, could also suggest a gastric outlet obstruction. However, the absence of intrinsic luminal lesions on upper gastrointestinal endoscopy and the specific finding of duodenal compression between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta make Superior Mesenteric Artery Syndrome a more likely diagnosis.\"\n\nNow matching analysis:\n\nGDR1: \"Gastric outlet obstruction was considered given vomiting and distension.\" PDR4, PDR5, PDR6 all explicitly state that symptoms could be consistent with gastric outlet obstruction. This is equivalent to considering it. No other PDR statements mention gastric outlet obstruction as a consideration. Match: PDR4, PDR5, PDR6.\n\nGDR2: \"Peptic stricture of the stomach was considered.\" No PDR statement mentions peptic stricture. No match.\n\nGDR3: \"Neoplasm of the stomach was considered.\" No PDR statement mentions neoplasm. No match.\n\nGDR4: \"Annular pancreas, diabetic gastroparesis, internal hernia, duodenal bezoar considered but excluded by endoscopy.\" PDR4, PDR5, PDR6 mention normal upper gastrointestinal endoscopy or absence of intrinsic luminal lesions, which directly indicates that other causes were ruled out. Although specific conditions are not named, the justification (endoscopy normal) is equivalent. Match: PDR4, PDR5, PDR6.\n\nGDR5: \"Peptic ulcer disease and duodenal tumors considered and excluded by imaging and endoscopy.\" Same reasoning as GDR4 – endoscopy normal excludes these. PDR4, PDR5, PDR6 match. Match: PDR4, PDR5, PDR6.\n\nGDR6: \"Scleroderma and other collagen/vascular disorders considered as mimics.\" No PDR statement mentions scleroderma or collagen disorders. No match.\n\nGDR7: \"Vascular compression due to reduced aortomesenteric angle supported by CT findings.\" All six PDR statements mention compression of the duodenum between the SMA and aorta (some specify acute angle, CT findings). This is equivalent to the CT-supported vascular compression. Match: PDR1, PDR2, PDR3, PDR4, PDR5, PDR6.\n\nSummary of matches:\n- GDR1: PDR4, PDR5, PDR6\n- GDR2: none\n- GDR3: none\n- GDR4: PDR4, PDR5, PDR6\n- GDR5: PDR4, PDR5, PDR6\n- GDR6: none\n- GDR7: PDR1, PDR2, PDR3, PDR4, PDR5, PDR6\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [\n      \"The patient's symptoms of vomiting, abdominal pain, and bloating, along with the finding of marked gastric distension, could also be consistent with a gastric outlet obstruction. However, the normal upper gastrointestinal endoscopy and the specific finding of compression of the duodenum between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta make Superior Mesenteric Artery Syndrome a more likely diagnosis.\",\n      \"The patient's symptoms of vomiting, abdominal pain, and bloating, along with the finding of marked gastric distension, could also be consistent with a gastric outlet obstruction. However, the normal upper gastrointestinal endoscopy and the specific finding of compression of the third portion of the duodenum between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta make Superior Mesenteric Artery Syndrome a more likely diagnosis.\",\n      \"The patient's symptoms of vomiting, abdominal pain, and bloating, along with the imaging findings of marked gastric distension, could also suggest a gastric outlet obstruction. However, the absence of intrinsic luminal lesions on upper gastrointestinal endoscopy and the specific finding of duodenal compression between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta make Superior Mesenteric Artery Syndrome a more likely diagnosis.\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": [\n      \"The patient's symptoms of vomiting, abdominal pain, and bloating, along with the finding of marked gastric distension, could also be consistent with a gastric outlet obstruction. However, the normal upper gastrointestinal endoscopy and