nd bloating, along with the imaging findings of marked gastric distension, could also suggest a gastric outlet obstruction. However, the absence of intrinsic luminal lesions on upper gastrointestinal endoscopy and the specific finding of duodenal compression between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta make Superior Mesenteric Artery Syndrome a more likely diagnosis.\"\n    ],\n    \"6\": [],\n    \"7\": [\n      \"The patient's symptoms of intractable vomiting, postprandial upper abdominal pain, and bloating, combined with the findings of marked gastric distension and compression of the third portion of the duodenum between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta at an acute angle on CT, are consistent with Superior Mesenteric Artery Syndrome. The patient's lean body habitus and recent fasting may have contributed to the compression.\",\n      \"The patient's symptoms of intractable vomiting, postprandial upper abdominal pain, and bloating, combined with the findings of marked gastric distension and compression of the third portion of the duodenum between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta at an acute angle on CT, are consistent with Superior Mesenteric Artery Syndrome. The patient's lean body habitus and recent history of fasting may have contributed to the development of this condition.\",\n      \"The patient's symptoms of intractable vomiting, postprandial upper abdominal pain, and bloating, combined with the imaging findings of marked gastric distension and compression of the third portion of the duodenum between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta, are consistent with Superior Mesenteric Artery Syndrome. The patient's lean body habitus and recent fasting may have contributed to the narrowing of the angle between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta, leading to duodenal compression.\",\n      \"The patient's symptoms of vomiting, abdominal pain, and bloating, along with the finding of marked gastric distension, could also be consistent with a gastric outlet obstruction. However, the normal upper gastrointestinal endoscopy and the specific finding of compression of the duodenum between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta make Superior Mesenteric Artery Syndrome a more likely diagnosis.\",\n      \"The patient's symptoms of vomiting, abdominal pain, and bloating, along with the finding of marked gastric distension, could also be consistent with a gastric outlet obstruction. However, the normal upper gastrointestinal endoscopy and the specific finding of compression of the third portion of the duodenum between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta make Superior Mesenteric Artery Syndrome a more likely diagnosis.\",\n      \"The patient's symptoms of vomiting, abdominal pain, and bloating, along with the imaging findings of marked gastric distension, could also suggest a gastric outlet obstruction. However, the absence of intrinsic luminal lesions on upper gastrointestinal endoscopy and the specific finding of duodenal compression between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta make Superior Mesenteric Artery Syndrome a more likely diagnosis.\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```",
