  "ca9e2caa37d297d95553073524e41d99d8ec2e64c4da9f7f3d99c94bcf9ac97f": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Consideration of extrahepatic HCC metastasis — “Extrahepatic HCC metastasis should be considered based on clinical manifestations, past medical history, and imaging analysis.”\n2. Consideration of primary soft tissue sarcoma — “Primary malignant soft tissue tumors or sarcomas cannot be completely excluded.”\n3. Exclusion of soft tissue sarcoma — “The tumor was negative for vimentin, directly excluding the possibility of mesenchymal origin soft tissue sarcomas or other tumors.”\n4. Confirmation of metastatic HCC by IHC — “The neoplastic cells were strongly positive for pan-CK, CK18… and HepPar-1.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\nP1: “The patient has a history of HBV-positive hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) and the presence of multiple nodules in the liver and a subcutaneous mass in the right flank, which is consistent with metastatic disease. The elevated alpha-fetoprotein level also supports this diagnosis.”\nP2: “The patient has a history of HBV-positive HCC and the imaging findings of multiple nodules in the liver and a large right-flank soft tissue mass invading the 12th rib are consistent with metastatic disease. The elevated alpha-fetoprotein level also supports this diagnosis.”\nP3: “The presence of a large subcutaneous mass in the right flank and the invasion of the 12th rib could be indicative of a primary soft tissue sarcoma. However, the patient's history of HCC and the presence of multiple liver nodules make metastatic HCC a more likely diagnosis.”\nP4: “The presence of a large subcutaneous mass and the imaging findings of a soft tissue mass invading the 12th rib could also be consistent with a primary soft tissue sarcoma. However, the patient's history of HCC and the presence of multiple liver nodules make metastatic HCC a more likely diagnosis.”\n\nMatching analysis for each Groundtruth statement:\n\n1. GT1: Consideration of extrahepatic HCC metastasis\n- Potential matches: P1, P2.\n- Pros: P1 and P2 both state that the patient has a history of HBV-positive HCC, liver nodules, and a subcutaneous/right-flank mass, and explicitly conclude these findings are “consistent with metastatic disease.” This is equivalent to considering extrahepatic HCC metastasis based on clinical manifestations, past medical history, and imaging.\n- Cons: P1 and P2 do not use the exact word “extrahepatic,” but the right-flank soft tissue mass is an extrahepatic site, so the meaning is equivalent.\n- Decision: Match P1 and P2.\n\n2. GT2: Consideration of primary soft tissue sarcoma\n- Potential matches: P3, P4.\n- Pros: P3 states the findings “could be indicative of a primary soft tissue sarcoma.” P4 states the findings “could also be consistent with a primary soft tissue sarcoma.” Both directly express that primary soft tissue sarcoma is a diagnostic consideration and cannot be fully excluded.\n- Cons: P3 and P4 ultimately favor metastatic HCC, but they still acknowledge soft tissue sarcoma as a possible alternative diagnosis. This matches the groundtruth statement that sarcoma “cannot be completely excluded.”\n- Decision: Match P3 and P4.\n\n3. GT3: Exclusion of soft tissue sarcoma\n- Potential matches: None.\n- Pros: P3 and P4 mention soft tissue sarcoma but do not exclude it based on vimentin negativity or any other definitive test.\n- Cons: No predicted statement mentions vimentin, immunohistochemistry, or direct exclusion of mesenchymal-origin sarcoma.\n- Decision: No match.\n\n4. GT4: Confirmation of metastatic HCC by IHC\n- Potential matches: None.\n- Pros: P1 and P2 mention elevated alpha-fetoprotein, which can support HCC.\n- Cons: They do not mention pan-CK, CK18, HepPar-1, or any immunohistochemical confirmation. Elevated AFP is not equivalent to IHC confirmation of HCC.\n- Decision: No match.\n\nSummary of matching decisions:\n- GT1 matches P1 and P2.\n- GT2 matches P3 and P4.\n- GT3 has no match.\n- GT4 has no match.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [\n      \"The patient has a history of HBV-positive hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) and the presence of multiple nodules in the liver and a subcutaneous mass in the right flank, which is consistent with metastatic disease. The elevated alpha-fetoprotein level also supports this diagnosis.\",\n      \"The patient has a history of HBV-positive HCC and the imaging findings of multiple nodules in the liver and a large right-flank soft tissue mass invading the 12th rib are consistent with metastatic disease. The elevated alpha-fetoprotein level also supports this diagnosis.\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [\n      \"The presence of a large subcutaneous mass in the right flank and the invasion of the 12th rib could be indicative of a primary soft tissue sarcoma. However, the patient's history of HCC and the presence of multiple liver nodules make metastatic HCC a more likely diagnosis.\",\n      \"The presence of a large subcutaneous mass and the imaging findings of a soft tissue mass invading the 12th rib could also be consistent with a primary soft tissue sarcoma. However, the patient's history of HCC and the presence of multiple liver nodules make metastatic HCC a more likely diagnosis.\"\n    ],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": []\n  }\n}\n```",
    "prompt_id": "mcr.reasoning_recall"
  },
