  "6a8b15f954084130836029a80da3890f22ce0a75524e8d7ec185dd30695ed7c4": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\n### Groundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Inflamed residual appendiceal stump considered because CT demonstrated a remaining stump and abscess — “swollen and thickened tubular structure measuring 36x27 mm, corresponding to a remaining appendiceal stump with a 25 mm collection adjacent to the clips.”\n2. Cecal diverticulitis considered due to similar right‐lower‐quadrant presentation — “One of the most common differential diagnoses for stump appendicitis is cecal diverticulitis. It should be considered due to its location.”\n3. Duplicated appendix considered owing to a rare anatomic anomaly that can cause post‐appendectomy appendicitis — “another conceivable cause of appendicitis following a prior appendectomy is the presence of a duplicated appendix. This anomaly is exceptionally rare, occurring in roughly 0.004% of patients…”\n\n### Predicted Diagnostic Reasons (all statements verbatim):\n- S1: \"The patient has a history of laparoscopic appendectomy, and the current presentation of right iliac fossa pain, fever, and elevated white-blood-cell count with neutrophilia suggests an infectious process. The abdominal CT scan showing a swollen and thickened tubular structure at the cecal pole with a pericecal collection adjacent to surgical clips is consistent with appendiceal stump appendicitis, which is a rare complication of appendectomy.\"\n- S2: \"The patient has a history of laparoscopic appendectomy, and the current presentation of right iliac fossa pain, fever, and elevated white-blood-cell count with neutrophilia suggests an infectious process. The abdominal CT scan showing a swollen and thickened tubular structure at the cecal pole with a pericecal collection adjacent to surgical clips is consistent with appendiceal stump appendicitis.\"\n- S3: \"The patient has a history of laparoscopic appendectomy, and the abdominal CT scan shows a swollen and thickened tubular structure at the cecal pole with a pericecal collection, which is consistent with an appendiceal abscess. The patient's symptoms, such as right iliac fossa pain, nausea, and diarrhea, also support this diagnosis. The elevated white-blood-cell count and C-reactive protein level indicate an infectious process.\"\n- S4: \"The patient has a history of laparoscopic appendectomy, and the abdominal CT scan shows a swollen and thickened tubular structure at the cecal pole with a pericecal collection, which is consistent with an appendiceal abscess. The patient's symptoms of right iliac fossa pain, nausea, and diarrhea, along with the elevated white-blood-cell count and C-reactive protein level, also support this diagnosis.\"\n- S5: \"The patient has a history of laparoscopic appendectomy, and the abdominal CT scan shows a swollen and thickened tubular structure at the cecal pole with a pericecal collection, which is consistent with an appendiceal abscess. The patient's symptoms, such as right iliac fossa pain, nausea, and diarrhea, also support this diagnosis.\"\n\n### Matching Analysis:\n\n**Groundtruth 1: Inflamed residual appendiceal stump considered because CT demonstrated a remaining stump and abscess.**\n- Potential matches: S1, S2, S3, S4, S5 all mention the CT finding of a swollen tubular structure and pericecal collection. However, S1 and S2 explicitly state that the CT finding is consistent with *appendiceal stump appendicitis*, which directly corresponds to the groundtruth's consideration of an inflamed residual appendiceal stump. S3, S4, S5 interpret the same finding as consistent with an *appendiceal abscess*, which is a different diagnostic entity. The groundtruth specifically mentions \"stump\" and \"abscess\" but the justification is for considering stump appendicitis, not abscess. Therefore, only S1 and S2 are equivalent.\n- Pros: S1 and S2 use similar language (\"swollen and thickened tubular structure\", \"pericecal collection adjacent to surgical clips\") and directly link to stump appendicitis. They also mention the rare complication aspect (S1 adds \"rare complication\" which aligns with the groundtruth's context).\n- Cons: S3-S5 are about abscess, not stump. No match.\n- Decision: Matches: S1, S2.\n\n**Groundtruth 2: Cecal diverticulitis considered due to similar right‐lower‐quadrant presentation.**\n- Potential matches: None of the predicted statements mention cecal diverticulitis, differential diagnoses, or right-lower-quadrant presentation as a differential. They all focus on stump appendicitis or abscess. No statement discusses alternative diagnoses.\n- Pros: None.\n- Cons: No relevant content.\n- Decision: No matches.\n\n**Groundtruth 3: Duplicated appendix considered owing to a rare anatomic anomaly.**\n- Potential matches: None of the predicted statements mention duplicated appendix, anatomic anomalies, or any rare causes of post-appendectomy appendicitis. They only discuss the current CT findings and symptoms.\n- Pros: None.\n- Cons: No relevant content.\n- Decision: No matches.\n\n### Final Matching Decisions:\n- Groundtruth 1 matches predicted statements S1 and S2.\n- Groundtruth 2 matches no predicted statements.\n- Groundtruth 3 matches no predicted statements.\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [\n      \"The patient has a history of laparoscopic appendectomy, and the current presentation of right iliac fossa pain, fever, and elevated white-blood-cell count with neutrophilia suggests an infectious process. The abdominal CT scan showing a swollen and thickened tubular structure at the cecal pole with a pericecal collection adjacent to surgical clips is consistent with appendiceal stump appendicitis, which is a rare complication of appendectomy.\",\n      \"The patient has a history of laparoscopic appendectomy, and the current presentation of right iliac fossa pain, fever, and elevated white-blood-cell count with neutrophilia suggests an infectious process. The abdominal CT scan showing a swollen and thickened tubular structure at the cecal pole with a pericecal collection adjacent to surgical clips is consistent with appendiceal stump appendicitis.\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": []\n  }\n}\n```",
