  "f0f1f4134330deaceb43ae26269303fa8cc47bc2a151c2e43cf1af7dda9f677c": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons (GDR):\n1. Corneal melanoma was considered because of the presence of pigmented stromal lesions — “Corneal pigmented lesions could also be caused by corneal melanoma or corneal melanin deposition caused by topical epinephrine therapy.”\n2. Corneal melanin deposition from topical epinephrine was considered for the same reason — “Corneal pigmented lesions could also be caused by corneal melanoma or corneal melanin deposition caused by topical epinephrine therapy.”\n3. Prior pine processionary infection was evaluated as a potential source but deemed unlikely — “Despite the patient's history of Thaumetopoea pityocampa infection, this is an unlikely source, due to no association with Exophiala xenobiotica being reported and the absence of infection reported at the time of DSAEK.”\n4. Keratitis by more common Exophiala species was considered given previous reports but excluded when culture identified a rare strain — “Few cases of keratitis caused by Exophiala spp. have been reported: E. dermatitidis keratitis after keratoplasty and after laser in situ keratomileusis, E. jeanselmei keratitis after minor trauma and soft contact lens use, and E. phaeomuriformis keratitis after severe ocular surface problems,” … “Diagnosis was established by a positive direct examination and culture (Figure 3) of the same fungus from the surgically obtained corneal button.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons (PDR):\n- (Fungal keratitis) Evidence: \"The patient's history of an eye infection and the presence of brown, multilobular lesions in the deep corneal stroma, as seen on slit-lamp biomicroscopy, are suggestive of fungal keratitis. The lack of conjunctival hyperemia, discharge, and epithelial defect also supports this diagnosis, as fungal keratitis can present with a relatively quiet eye.\"\n- (Fungal keratitis) Evidence: \"The patient's history of eye infection and the presence of brown, multilobular lesions in the deep corneal stroma, as well as the central infiltrate involving all corneal layers on optical coherence tomography, are consistent with fungal keratitis. The lack of conjunctival hyperemia, discharge, and epithelial defect also suggests a fungal etiology.\"\n- (Fungal keratitis) Evidence: \"The patient's history of eye infection and the presence of brown, multilobular lesions in the deep corneal stroma, as seen on slit-lamp biomicroscopy, are suggestive of fungal keratitis. The lack of conjunctival hyperemia, discharge, and epithelial defect also supports this diagnosis, as fungal keratitis can present with a relatively quiet eye.\" (duplicate)\n- (Nocardia keratitis) Evidence: \"The patient's history of prior eye infection and the presence of multilobular lesions in the deep corneal stroma, as well as the findings on anterior segment optical coherence tomography, could also be consistent with Nocardia keratitis. However, the absence of a clear history of trauma or exposure to soil or organic matter makes this diagnosis less likely than fungal keratitis.\"\n- (Nocardia keratitis) Evidence: \"The patient's history of corticosteroid use and the presence of multilobular lesions in the deep corneal stroma could also be consistent with Nocardia keratitis. However, the absence of a clear history of trauma or exposure to soil or water, which are common risk factors for Nocardia infection, makes this diagnosis less likely than fungal keratitis.\"\n\nNow, for each GDR statement, consider potential matches from PDR:\n\nGDR1: Corneal melanoma considered. PDR do not mention corneal melanoma, pigmented lesions, or any differential diagnosis involving melanoma. All PDR focus on fungal and Nocardia keratitis. No match.\n- Pros: None.\n- Cons: No overlap in content.\n\nGDR2: Corneal melanin deposition from topical epinephrine considered. Same as above – no mention of epinephrine, melanin deposition, or topical therapy. No match.\n- Pros: None.\n- Cons: No overlap.\n\nGDR3: Prior pine processionary infection evaluated but deemed unlikely. PDR mention \"history of eye infection\" but do not specify pine processionary or Thaumetopoea pityocampa. They also do not discuss evaluating or ruling out this specific infection. No match.\n- Pros: None.\n- Cons: No specific mention.\n\nGDR4: Keratitis by common Exophiala species considered but excluded by culture. PDR discuss fungal keratitis generally but do not mention Exophiala species, previous reports of keratitis by specific species, or culture identification of a rare strain. The PDR statements are about clinical features suggestive of fungal keratitis, not about the diagnostic reasoning of considering common species and then excluding them. No match.\n- Pros: Both involve fungal keratitis, but the reasoning is different.\n- Cons: The PDR do not reference the specific differential of Exophiala species or the culture result.\n\nSummary of matching decisions: No PDR statement matches any GDR statement. All matches are empty.\n\nJSON output:\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": []\n  }\n}",
    "prompt_id": "mcr.reasoning_recall"
  },
