  "a18f0a84e528a778db455631d99579799998e515184d7376fc7b4928dba6b559": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Upper urinary tract urothelial carcinoma was suspected because “Typical symptoms are gross hematuria (80%) and flank pain (30%)” and imaging showed “a tubular filling defect with partial obstruction of the right mid ureter.”\n2. Ureteral fibroepithelial polyp was considered given that “IVU or retrograde pyelography typically demonstrates a tubular filling defect. This finding combined with negative cytology and biopsy should suspect a UFP.”\n3. Other benign ureteral lesions, including endometriomas, fibromas, leiomyomas, granulomas, neurofibromas, hemangiomas, and lymphangiomas, were contemplated because “Other benign lesions of the upper urinary tract include endometriomas, fibromas, leiomyomas, granulomas, neurofibromas, hemangiomas, and lymphangiomas.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n- Statement A (from Ureteral fibroepithelial polyp): \"The patient's presentation with gross hematuria and intermittent right pelvic pain, combined with the imaging findings of a tubular filling defect and a pedunculated ureteral tumor, is consistent with a ureteral fibroepithelial polyp. The negative urine cytology and endoscopic biopsy results also support this diagnosis, as fibroepithelial polyps are typically benign.\"\n- Statement B (from Ureteral fibroepithelial polyp): \"The patient's presentation with gross hematuria and intermittent right pelvic pain, combined with imaging findings of a tubular filling defect and a pedunculated ureteral tumor, is consistent with a ureteral fibroepithelial polyp. The lack of malignancy on urine cytology and endoscopic biopsy also supports this diagnosis.\"\n- Statement C (from Ureteral tumor): \"Although the biopsy results were negative for malignancy, the presence of a 5 cm long tubular mass in the mid ureter and the patient's significant smoking history increase the suspicion for a ureteral tumor, possibly a low-grade urothelial carcinoma. Further evaluation and follow-up are necessary to rule out malignancy.\"\n- Statement D (from Ureteral tumor): \"The patient's 60-pack-year smoking history increases the risk of urothelial carcinoma, and the presence of a 5 cm long tubular mass in the mid ureter with a vulnerable surface on flexible ureteroscopy is consistent with a ureteral tumor. Although urine cytology and endoscopic biopsy were negative for malignancy, these tests can have false-negative results, especially for low-grade tumors.\"\n\nMatching analysis:\n\nFor Groundtruth 1 (urothelial carcinoma suspicion):\n- Potential matches: Statements C and D mention suspicion for urothelial carcinoma. However, they do not cite the typical symptoms (gross hematuria, flank pain) or the specific imaging finding of a tubular filling defect with partial obstruction. Instead, they rely on smoking history and a tubular mass. The groundtruth reason explicitly uses symptoms and filling defect as justification. Therefore, no predicted statement provides equivalent justification. No match.\n\nFor Groundtruth 2 (fibroepithelial polyp consideration):\n- Potential matches: Statements A and B both explicitly state that the presentation (gross hematuria, flank pain), imaging (tubular filling defect), and negative cytology/biopsy support a ureteral fibroepithelial polyp. This directly matches the groundtruth reasoning that a tubular filling defect combined with negative cytology and biopsy should suspect a UFP. Both statements are equivalent justifications. Match for both A and B.\n\nFor Groundtruth 3 (other benign lesions):\n- Potential matches: No predicted statement mentions any of the listed benign lesions (endometriomas, fibromas, etc.). The predicted reasons only discuss fibroepithelial polyp and urothelial carcinoma. No match.\n\nSummary of matching decisions:\n- Groundtruth 1: no matches\n- Groundtruth 2: matches with Statement A and Statement B\n- Groundtruth 3: no matches\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [],\n    \"2\": [\n      \"The patient's presentation with gross hematuria and intermittent right pelvic pain, combined with the imaging findings of a tubular filling defect and a pedunculated ureteral tumor, is consistent with a ureteral fibroepithelial polyp. The negative urine cytology and endoscopic biopsy results also support this diagnosis, as fibroepithelial polyps are typically benign.\",\n      \"The patient's presentation with gross hematuria and intermittent right pelvic pain, combined with imaging findings of a tubular filling defect and a pedunculated ureteral tumor, is consistent with a ureteral fibroepithelial polyp. The lack of malignancy on urine cytology and endoscopic biopsy also supports this diagnosis.\"\n    ],\n    \"3\": []\n  }\n}",
    "prompt_id": "mcr.reasoning_recall"
  },
