  "7869f9c8eeff59b349c77705a73700892be12c12f6fffaf5eb81bd4f473b73e3": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons (G1-G7):\n1. Considered cauda equina syndrome or epidural abscess — “An initial differential diagnosis of cauda equina syndrome or epidural abscess was considered in the ED based on the presenting features of left leg pain and weakness, urinary incontinence and raised inflammatory markers. An urgent magnetic resonance image (MRI) of the lumbar spine was performed, which showed mild foraminal narrowing at L5-S1 with no central spinal canal stenosis and no evidence of epidural abscess.”\n2. Considered sepsis — “A chest X-ray, atypical pneumonia serology, urine microscopy and culture were all unremarkable.”\n3. Considered hip fracture — “A plain X-ray film of the left hip showed no fracture.”\n4. Considered malignancy — “His weight loss pointed towards the possibility of a malignancy, but normal tumour markers with lack of specific findings on MRI hip and isotope bone scan ruled out this possibility.”\n5. Considered septic arthritis or crystal arthropathy — “A differential diagnosis of septic arthritis and crystal arthropathy was considered, but an attempt to aspirate the left hip joint was unsuccessful.”\n6. Considered statin-induced myopathy — “Atorvastatin was ceased due to its potential to cause myositis and myalgia, although creatine kinase levels were within normal limits.”\n7. Distinction between infective and inflammatory myositis — “These findings of muscular oedema with enhancement favoured inflammatory, as opposed to infective, aetiology.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons (P1-P6):\nP1: \"Diagnosis: Iliopsoas tendinitis or iliopsoas syndrome\"\nP2: \"Evidence: The patient's symptoms of left leg pain and weakness, impaired mobility, and the MRI findings of bilateral iliacus and iliopsoas muscle oedema with enhancement and a left-sided insertional tendinopathy support this diagnosis.\"\nP3: \"The patient's response to treatment and the absence of other findings such as malignancy or infection also point towards this diagnosis.\"\nP4: \"Diagnosis: Polymyalgia Rheumatica\"\nP5: \"Evidence: The patient's symptoms of hip pain, weakness, and elevated inflammatory markers, along with the MRI findings of iliacus and iliopsoas muscle oedema, are consistent with polymyalgia rheumatica.\"\nP6: \"The absence of malignancy and other conditions also supports this diagnosis.\"\n\nNow, for each G, consider potential matches:\n\nG1: No predicted statement directly addresses cauda equina syndrome or epidural abscess. P2 mentions MRI findings but of iliacus/iliopsoas, not lumbar spine. No match. Pros: None. Cons: No equivalence.\n\nG2: Considered sepsis. P3 mentions \"absence of other findings such as malignancy or infection\" – infection includes sepsis. P6 mentions \"absence of malignancy and other conditions\" – other conditions could include sepsis. Both indicate that infection was ruled out, which is the essence of G2 (tests unremarkable). Pros: Both statements convey that infection was absent, matching the conclusion of G2. Cons: G2 specifies the tests performed; predicted statements are more general. However, the core reasoning (sepsis ruled out) is equivalent. Match: P3 and P6.\n\nG3: No predicted statement mentions hip fracture or X-ray. No match.\n\nG4: Considered malignancy. P3 explicitly mentions \"absence of other findings such as malignancy or infection\" – malignancy is directly stated. P6 says \"absence of malignancy and other conditions\" – malignancy is directly stated. Both match the ruling out of malignancy. Pros: Direct mention of malignancy absence. Cons: G4 includes specific tests (tumour markers, MRI, bone scan); predicted statements are general. But the key point (malignancy ruled out) is present. Match: P3 and P6.\n\nG5: Considered septic arthritis or crystal arthropathy. P3 mentions \"infection\" which could include septic arthritis, but does not mention crystal arthropathy. P6 mentions \"other conditions\" but is vague. The groundtruth also notes an unsuccessful aspiration, not a definitive ruling out. The predicted statements imply absence of infection, but the groundtruth does not confirm absence (aspiration failed). Pros: Partial overlap on infection. Cons: Crystal arthropathy not addressed; the groundtruth is a consideration, not a definitive exclusion. The match is weak and not equivalent. No match.\n\nG6: No predicted statement mentions statin-induced myopathy or creatine kinase. No match.\n\nG7: Distinction between infective and inflammatory myositis. P2 mentions \"MRI findings of bilateral iliacus and iliopsoas muscle oedema with enhancement\" – this directly matches the \"muscular oedema with enhancement\" in G7. P5 mentions \"MRI findings of iliacus and iliopsoas muscle oedema\" – also matches, though without \"enhancement\" explicitly. Both support the inflammatory aetiology (since the diagnoses are inflammatory conditions). Pros: Direct mention of the same MRI finding that favoured inflammatory over infective. Cons: P5 lacks \"enhancement\" but still refers to the same oedema. Match: P2 and P5.\n\nSummary of matching decisions:\n- G1: no match\n- G2: match with P3 and P6\n- G3: no match\n- G4: match with P3 and P6\n- G5: no match\n- G6: no match\n- G7: match with P2 and P5\n\nNow, JSON output with exact verbatim statements from predicted diagnostic reasons.\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [],\n    \"2\": [\n      \"The patient's response to treatment and the absence of other findings such as malignancy or infection also point towards this diagnosis.\",\n      \"The absence of malignancy and other conditions also supports this diagnosis.\"\n    ],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": [\n      \"The patient's response to treatment and the absence of other findings such as malignancy or infection also point towards this diagnosis.\",\n      \"The absence of malignancy and other conditions also supports this diagnosis.\"\n    ],\n    \"5\": [],\n    \"6\": [],\n    \"7\": [\n      \"Evidence: The patient's symptoms of left leg pain and weakness, impaired mobility, and the MRI findings of bilateral iliacus and iliopsoas muscle oedema with enhancement and a left-sided insertional tendinopathy support this diagnosis.\",\n      \"Evidence: The patient's symptoms of hip pain, weakness, and elevated inflammatory markers, along with the MRI findings of iliacus and iliopsoas muscle oedema, are consistent with polymyalgia rheumatica.\"\n    ]\n  }\n}",
    "prompt_id": "mcr.reasoning_recall"
