  "4ab7c7a8695cd78f6ef7dd595deaea7d2443ed289afa82ac6043626f65fc9027": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. \"Liver adenoma was initially considered given prior biopsies—'The primary differential diagnosis was bleeding episode of liver adenoma—misled by the previously obtained histopathology report.'\"\n2. \"Hepatocellular carcinoma was suspected because of persistent hypervascular lesions with necrosis—'Because of a suspected atypical hepatocellular carcinoma, an additional magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) study was performed … and a surgical resection was indicated.'\"\n3. \"Hepatic-origin tumors were excluded by immunohistochemistry negative for liver markers—'Immunohistochemical staining revealed … and lack of reaction against … Arginase-1, for exclusion of a liver-own tumor.'\"\n4. \"Metastatic liver tumor and intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma were considered in adults with hypervascular hepatic masses—'In adults its differential diagnoses can be metastatic liver tumor or intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma.'\"\n5. \"Liver hematoma was part of the differential given imaging evidence of hemorrhage—'Preoperative CT-scan … showing hypervascularized hepatic lesions with central necrosis and associated hematoma.'\"\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons (individual statements):\nA. \"The patient has a history of hypervascular liver lesions in segment IV, which were biopsied and shown to be adenomas. The current presentation with acute-onset abdominal pain, anemia, and imaging findings of subcapsular hepatic bleeding and active bleeding from one of the lesions is consistent with hemorrhage from a hepatic adenoma.\"\nB. \"Although the initial biopsies showed adenomas without malignancy, the persistence of hypervascular lesions and the development of central necrosis on MRI raise suspicion for an atypical hepatocellular carcinoma. The planned elective surgical resection is also consistent with this diagnosis.\"\nC. \"The patient's lesions have atypical features, including central necrosis on MRI, which raises suspicion for an atypical hepatocellular carcinoma. The lack of typical features of hepatocellular carcinoma on CT and normal tumor marker levels do not rule out this diagnosis, and the planned elective surgical resection is likely aimed at obtaining a definitive diagnosis.\"\nD. \"The patient's lesions have atypical features, including hypervascularity and central necrosis on MRI, which raises suspicion for an atypical hepatocellular carcinoma. The lack of typical features of hepatocellular carcinoma on CT and normal tumor marker levels do not entirely rule out this diagnosis, and the planned surgical resection is likely aimed at obtaining a definitive diagnosis.\"\n\nMatching analysis:\n\nGroundtruth 1:\n- Potential matches: A and B.\n  - Pros: A explicitly states history of adenomas and hemorrhage, directly matching the initial consideration of bleeding adenoma. B also mentions initial biopsies showed adenomas, which is part of the justification.\n  - Cons: B does not mention hemorrhage, but the groundtruth includes the misleading histopathology; B acknowledges the adenomas but then shifts to HCC suspicion. Still, it supports the initial adenoma diagnosis.\n- Decision: Both A and B match.\n\nGroundtruth 2:\n- Potential matches: B, C, D.\n  - Pros: All three mention suspicion of atypical HCC due to persistent hypervascular lesions and central necrosis on MRI, and reference surgical resection, exactly matching the groundtruth.\n  - Cons: None.\n- Decision: B, C, and D all match.\n\nGroundtruth 3:\n- Potential matches: None of the predicted statements mention immunohistochemistry, Arginase-1, or exclusion of hepatic-origin tumors.\n- Decision: No match (empty array).\n\nGroundtruth 4:\n- Potential matches: None of the predicted statements mention metastatic liver tumor or intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma as differentials.\n- Decision: No match (empty array).\n\nGroundtruth 5:\n- Potential matches: A.\n  - Pros: A explicitly mentions subcapsular hepatic bleeding and active bleeding, which is equivalent to hematoma, and the imaging findings of hemorrhage.\n  - Cons: B, C, D mention central necrosis but not hematoma/hemorrhage.\n- Decision: Only A matches.\n\nFinal matching decisions:\n- Groundtruth 1: [A, B]\n- Groundtruth 2: [B, C, D]\n- Groundtruth 3: []\n- Groundtruth 4: []\n- Groundtruth 5: [A]\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [\n      \"The patient has a history of hypervascular liver lesions in segment IV, which were biopsied and shown to be adenomas. The current presentation with acute-onset abdominal pain, anemia, and imaging findings of subcapsular hepatic bleeding and active bleeding from one of the lesions is consistent with hemorrhage from a hepatic adenoma.\",\n      \"Although the initial biopsies showed adenomas without malignancy, the persistence of hypervascular lesions and the development of central necrosis on MRI raise suspicion for an atypical hepatocellular carcinoma. The planned elective surgical resection is also consistent with this diagnosis.\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [\n      \"Although the initial biopsies showed adenomas without malignancy, the persistence of hypervascular lesions and the development of central necrosis on MRI raise suspicion for an atypical hepatocellular carcinoma. The planned elective surgical resection is also consistent with this diagnosis.\",\n      \"The patient's lesions have atypical features, including central necrosis on MRI, which raises suspicion for an atypical hepatocellular carcinoma. The lack of typical features of hepatocellular carcinoma on CT and normal tumor marker levels do not rule out this diagnosis, and the planned elective surgical resection is likely aimed at obtaining a definitive diagnosis.\",\n      \"The patient's lesions have atypical features, including hypervascularity and central necrosis on MRI, which raises suspicion for an atypical hepatocellular carcinoma. The lack of typical features of hepatocellular carcinoma on CT and normal tumor marker levels do not entirely rule out this diagnosis, and the planned surgical resection is likely aimed at obtaining a definitive diagnosis.\"\n    ],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": [],\n    \"5\": [\n      \"The patient has a history of hypervascular liver lesions in segment IV, which were biopsied and shown to be adenomas. The current presentation with acute-onset abdominal pain, anemia, and imaging findings of subcapsular hepatic bleeding and active bleeding from one of the lesions is consistent with hemorrhage from a hepatic adenoma.\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```",
