  "a65ce2a99895abefa16f0a4b0846b3f085f00989c379472ad1ead4badfc131a0": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. \"Consideration of urinoma—“Multiple slightly strong echoes, septations, and flocculent echoes were more likely to be seen in ultrasonography of hematoma.”\"\n2. \"Inclusion of lymphangioma and mesenteric cyst—“The differential diagnosis of cystic masses located between the kidney and spine includes urinoma, lymphangioma, mesenteric cyst, and so on.”\"\n3. \"Exclusion of renal tumor (eg, angiomyolipoma)—“Literature research indicated that renal tumors were the most common cause of WS, and hydronephrosis was an uncommon cause.”\"\n4. \"Identification of hydronephrosis secondary to bladder dysfunction as the hemorrhage cause—“In the present case, hydronephrosis was believed to be the direct cause of WS.”\"\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n- \"The prenatal ultrasound findings of bilateral renal enlargement, hydronephrosis, and a dilated fetal bladder, along with the postnatal MRI and magnetic resonance urography results showing bladder wall irregularity and thickening, bilateral ureteral dilation, and urinary retention, are consistent with PUV. The elevated serum creatinine level also indicates impaired renal function, which is a common complication of PUV.\"\n- \"The prenatal ultrasound findings of bilateral renal enlargement and hydronephrosis, a markedly dilated fetal bladder, and oligohydramnios, along with the postnatal MRI and magnetic resonance urography results showing bladder wall irregularity and thickening, bilateral ureteral dilation, and urinary retention, are consistent with PUV. The elevated serum creatinine level also indicates impaired renal function, which is a common complication of PUV.\"\n- \"The prenatal ultrasound findings of bilateral renal enlargement and hydronephrosis, a markedly dilated fetal bladder, and oligohydramnios, combined with the postnatal MRI and magnetic resonance urography results showing bladder wall irregularity and thickening, bilateral ureteral dilation, and urinary retention, are consistent with PUV. The elevated serum creatinine level also indicates impaired renal function, which is a common complication of PUV.\"\n- \"All panel members agree that the prenatal ultrasound findings of bilateral renal enlargement, hydronephrosis, and a dilated fetal bladder, along with the postnatal MRI and magnetic resonance urography results showing bladder wall irregularity and thickening, bilateral ureteral dilation, and urinary retention, are consistent with PUV. The elevated serum creatinine level also indicates impaired renal function, which is a common complication of PUV.\"\n- \"The prenatal ultrasound findings of bilateral renal enlargement, hydronephrosis, and a dilated fetal bladder, as well as the postnatal MRI and magnetic resonance urography results, could also be consistent with Prune Belly Syndrome. However, the absence of characteristic abdominal muscle deficiency and undescended testes in the provided information makes PUV a more likely diagnosis.\"\n- \"The prenatal ultrasound findings of bilateral renal enlargement and hydronephrosis, a markedly dilated fetal bladder, and the postnatal MRI and magnetic resonance urography results showing bladder wall irregularity and thickening, and bilateral ureteral dilation, could also be consistent with Prune Belly Syndrome. However, the presence of a left perirenal fluid collection and the absence of typical abdominal muscle deficiency make PUV a more likely diagnosis.\"\n- \"The presence of bilateral renal enlargement and hydronephrosis, a dilated bladder, and a perirenal fluid collection, along with the postnatal findings of impaired renal function and urinary retention, could also suggest Prune Belly Syndrome, a rare congenital disorder characterized by absent or weakened abdominal muscles, undescended testes, and urinary tract anomalies.\"\n- \"The prenatal ultrasound findings of bilateral renal enlargement and hydronephrosis, a markedly dilated fetal bladder, and the postnatal MRI and magnetic resonance urography results showing bladder wall irregularity and thickening, and bilateral ureteral dilation, could also be consistent with Prune Belly Syndrome. However, the presence of a left perirenal fluid collection and the absence of other characteristic features such as abdominal muscle weakness and undescended testes make PUV a more likely diagnosis.\"\n- \"While all panel members consider Prune Belly Syndrome as a possible diagnosis due to the prenatal ultrasound findings of bilateral renal enlargement, hydronephrosis, and a dilated fetal bladder, as well as the postnatal MRI and magnetic resonance urography results, they note that the absence of characteristic abdominal muscle deficiency and undescended testes makes PUV a more likely diagnosis.\"\n\nFor each groundtruth statement, potential matches from predicted reasons:\n\nStatement 1: Consideration of urinoma with specific ultrasound findings. Predicted reasons do not mention urinoma, echoes, septations, or hematoma. No match. Pros: none. Cons: completely different topic.\n\nStatement 2: Inclusion of lymphangioma and mesenteric cyst in differential of cystic masses between kidney and spine. Predicted reasons do not mention these diagnoses. They discuss PUV and Prune Belly Syndrome, which are congenital urinary tract obstructions, not cystic masses in that location. No match.\n\nStatement 3: Exclusion of renal tumor (e.g., angiomyolipoma) as cause of WS. Predicted reasons do not mention renal tumors, angiomyolipoma, or WS (Wunderlich syndrome). They discuss causes of hydronephrosis and bladder dysfunction. No match.\n\nStatement 4: Identification of hydronephrosis secondary to bladder dysfunction as hemorrhage cause. Predicted reasons mention hydronephrosis and bladder dysfunction (bladder wall irregularity, thickening, urinary retention) but in the context of PUV and Prune Belly Syndrome, not as a cause of hemorrhage/WS. The groundtruth statement specifically says \"hydronephrosis secondary to bladder dysfunction as the hemorrhage cause\" and references \"WS\". The predicted reasons do not mention hemorrhage or WS. While they mention hydronephrosis and bladder issues, the context and purpose are different. No equivalent justification for the diagnosis of WS. Thus no match.\n\nSummary: No predicted diagnostic reason statement matches any groundtruth diagnostic reason. All arrays are empty.\n\nJSON output:\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": []\n  }\n}\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": []\n  }\n}\n```",
