  "e48ab1c0c37b1362793c62be129673fab48e50bc95e11c252a2b07569f3dee32": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Spindle cell squamous cell carcinoma was favored because p63 positivity indicates epithelial differentiation—“only p63 was detected in the spindle cell nuclei.”\n2. Post-irradiation sarcoma was considered given the patient’s remote chemoradiotherapy—“SCSCC and post-irradiation sarcoma were considered in the differential diagnosis, given that the patient had a history of chemoradiotherapy for treating SCC of the buccal mucosa 15 years before.”\n3. High-grade sarcoma (undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma) was suspected due to absence of lineage differentiation—“High-grade sarcoma, including undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma, was strongly suspected as a mesenchymal neoplasm, given no obvious evidence of osteogenic, muscular, or adipogenic differentiation.”\n4. Malignant melanoma was ruled out because of negative S-100 staining—“The negative S-100 immunoreactivity indicated that a malignant melanoma was less likely, because S-100 positivity is observed in more than 90% of those, including amelanotic melanoma.”\n5. Radiation-induced stromal atypia was considered based on history but lacked supportive atypical features—“radiation-induced stromal atypia was also considered as a differential diagnosis.”\n6. Reactive spindle cell lesions were excluded by evidence of high proliferative and mutant p53 activity—“reactive spindle cell lesions could be ruled out because of the strong expression of both p53 and Ki-67 in our patient’s tumor.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons (list of evidence statements):\nP1: \"The presence of a spindle cell population with atypical features, including bizarre nuclei and atypical mitoses, alongside a history of squamous cell carcinoma, suggests a sarcomatoid transformation. Immunohistochemical staining showing positivity for p63 and p53 supports this diagnosis, as these markers are often expressed in squamous cell carcinomas.\"\nP2: \"The patient's history of squamous cell carcinoma, the presence of a polypoid mass with a whitish pseudomembrane, and the biopsy findings of atypical spindle and pleomorphic cells with positive immunohistochemical staining for p63 and p53 support this diagnosis. The high Ki-67 labeling index also suggests a malignant process.\"\nP3: \"The consensus among the panel, including the pathologist and oncologist, supports this diagnosis due to the patient's history of squamous cell carcinoma, the presence of a polypoid mass, and biopsy findings of atypical spindle and pleomorphic cells with positive immunohistochemical staining for p63 and p53.\"\nP4: \"The patient's history of chemoradiotherapy for squamous cell carcinoma, the presence of a polypoid mass with a whitish pseudomembrane, and the biopsy findings of atypical spindle and pleomorphic cells with positive immunohistochemical staining for vimentin, α-smooth muscle actin, p63, p53, and CD68, but negative for epithelial markers, suggest a malignant spindle cell process, most likely a sarcoma.\"\nP5: \"This diagnosis is considered due to the biopsy findings of atypical spindle and pleomorphic cells with positive immunohistochemical staining for vimentin, α-smooth muscle actin, p63, p53, and CD68, but negative for epithelial markers, suggesting a malignant spindle cell process.\"\n\nMatching analysis for each Groundtruth statement:\n\n- G1: p63 positivity indicating epithelial differentiation. Predicted statements P1, P2, and P3 all mention p63 positivity as supporting sarcomatoid squamous cell carcinoma (or equivalent). P1 explicitly states \"p63 and p53 supports this diagnosis, as these markers are often expressed in squamous cell carcinomas.\" P2 and P3 also mention p63/p53 positivity. These are equivalent justifications. Pros: Direct mention of p63. Cons: None. Match: P1, P2, P3.\n\n- G2: Post-irradiation sarcoma considered due to remote chemoradiotherapy. Predicted statement P4 explicitly mentions \"history of chemoradiotherapy for squamous cell carcinoma\" and suggests \"most likely a sarcoma.\" P5 does not mention history. Pros: Direct mention of chemoradiotherapy history. Cons: None. Match: P4.\n\n- G3: High-grade sarcoma (undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma) suspected due to absence of lineage differentiation. No predicted statement mentions absence of osteogenic, muscular, or adipogenic differentiation. Predicted statements only mention negative for epithelial markers, which is different. No match.\n\n- G4: Malignant melanoma ruled out due to negative S-100. No predicted statement mentions S-100. No match.\n\n- G5: Radiation-induced stromal atypia considered. No predicted statement mentions this differential. No match.\n\n- G6: Reactive spindle cell lesions excluded by high p53 and Ki-67. Predicted statement P2 mentions \"high Ki-67 labeling index also suggests a malignant process\" and also includes p53 positivity. This is equivalent to using high proliferative activity and p53 to rule out reactive lesions. P1 mentions p53 but not Ki-67; P3 mentions p53 but not Ki-67; P4/P5 do not mention Ki-67. Pros: Direct mention of Ki-67 and p53 in context of malignant process. Cons: None. Match: P2.\n\nFinal matching decisions:\n- G1: P1, P2, P3\n- G2: P4\n- G3: (none)\n- G4: (none)\n- G5: (none)\n- G6: P2\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [\n      \"The presence of a spindle cell population with atypical features, including bizarre nuclei and atypical mitoses, alongside a history of squamous cell carcinoma, suggests a sarcomatoid transformation. Immunohistochemical staining showing positivity for p63 and p53 supports this diagnosis, as these markers are often expressed in squamous cell carcinomas.\",\n      \"The patient's history of squamous cell carcinoma, the presence of a polypoid mass with a whitish pseudomembrane, and the biopsy findings of atypical spindle and pleomorphic cells with positive immunohistochemical staining for p63 and p53 support this diagnosis. The high Ki-67 labeling index also suggests a malignant process.\",\n      \"The consensus among the panel, including the pathologist and oncologist, supports this diagnosis due to the patient's history of squamous cell carcinoma, the presence of a polypoid mass, and biopsy findings of atypical spindle and pleomorphic cells with positive immunohistochemical staining for p63 and p53.\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [\n      \"The patient's history of chemoradiotherapy for squamous cell carcinoma, the presence of a polypoid mass with a whitish pseudomembrane, and the biopsy findings of atypical spindle and pleomorphic cells with positive immunohistochemical staining for vimentin, α-smooth muscle actin, p63, p53, and CD68, but negative for epithelial markers, suggest a malignant spindle cell process, most likely a sarcoma.\"\n    ],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": [],\n    \"5\": [],\n    \"6\": [\n      \"The patient's history of squamous cell carcinoma, the presence of a polypoid mass with a whitish pseudomembrane, and the biopsy findings of atypical spindle and pleomorphic cells with positive immunohistochemical staining for p63 and p53 support this diagnosis. The high Ki-67 labeling index also suggests a malignant process.\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```",
