  "c16d08da2ab9b67509faa827bf7875388daacc8e2eb9a198d290beef3cc9958a": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Consideration of invasive squamous cell carcinoma — “histologic examination showed lobules and detached aggregations of eosinophilic keratinocytes with pleomorphic nuclei and some prominent nucleoli infiltrating the reticular dermis…a diagnosis of invasive SCC was taken.”\n2. Consideration of basal cell carcinoma for residual papules — “some papular lesions with the dermoscopic features of BCC.”\n3. Diagnosis of basosquamous carcinoma after immunohistochemical revision — “revision of the first histologic exam highlighted the presence of basaloid cells through BerEP4 and Bcl-2 immunohistochemistry…coexistence of both the squamous and basaloid components.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons (list of all individual statements):\n- \"The excisional biopsy of the lesion showed lobules and detached aggregates of eosinophilic keratinocytes with pleomorphic nuclei and prominent nucleoli infiltrating the reticular dermis, which is consistent with invasive cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma.\"\n- \"The excisional biopsy of the lesion showed lobules and detached aggregates of eosinophilic keratinocytes with pleomorphic nuclei and prominent nucleoli infiltrating the reticular dermis, which is consistent with invasive cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma. The patient's response to cemiplimab, a PD-1 inhibitor, also supports this diagnosis.\"\n- \"Dermoscopic evaluation of the papules that remained after treatment revealed features typical of basal cell carcinoma.\"\n- \"Dermoscopic evaluation of the papular lesions that remained after treatment with cemiplimab revealed features typical of basal cell carcinoma, suggesting that the patient may have a second primary skin cancer.\"\n- \"Dermoscopic evaluation of the papular lesions that remained after treatment revealed features typical of basal cell carcinoma, suggesting that the patient may have a second primary skin cancer.\"\n\nNow evaluate each groundtruth statement:\n\nGroundtruth 1 (invasive SCC): The predicted statements that mention the same histologic description are the first two. Both describe the excisional biopsy showing lobules and detached aggregates of eosinophilic keratinocytes with pleomorphic nuclei and prominent nucleoli infiltrating the reticular dermis. This directly matches the groundtruth description. The second statement adds an extra detail about cemiplimab response, but the core histologic evidence is the same. Thus both are matches.\n\nGroundtruth 2 (BCC for residual papules): The predicted statements about dermoscopic evaluation of papules/papular lesions revealing features typical of BCC are all three. They all convey the same essential information: dermoscopic features of BCC in residual papules. The second and third add a suggestion of second primary cancer, but that does not negate the match. Thus all three are matches.\n\nGroundtruth 3 (basosquamous carcinoma after immunohistochemistry): None of the predicted statements mention basosquamous carcinoma, immunohistochemical markers (BerEP4, Bcl-2), or coexistence of squamous and basaloid components. The predicted reasons only discuss SCC and BCC separately. No match.\n\nFinal matching decisions:\n- Groundtruth 1 matches predicted statements 1 and 2.\n- Groundtruth 2 matches predicted statements 3, 4, and 5.\n- Groundtruth 3 matches none.\n\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [\n      \"The excisional biopsy of the lesion showed lobules and detached aggregates of eosinophilic keratinocytes with pleomorphic nuclei and prominent nucleoli infiltrating the reticular dermis, which is consistent with invasive cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma.\",\n      \"The excisional biopsy of the lesion showed lobules and detached aggregates of eosinophilic keratinocytes with pleomorphic nuclei and prominent nucleoli infiltrating the reticular dermis, which is consistent with invasive cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma. The patient's response to cemiplimab, a PD-1 inhibitor, also supports this diagnosis.\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [\n      \"Dermoscopic evaluation of the papules that remained after treatment revealed features typical of basal cell carcinoma.\",\n      \"Dermoscopic evaluation of the papular lesions that remained after treatment with cemiplimab revealed features typical of basal cell carcinoma, suggesting that the patient may have a second primary skin cancer.\",\n      \"Dermoscopic evaluation of the papular lesions that remained after treatment revealed features typical of basal cell carcinoma, suggesting that the patient may have a second primary skin cancer.\"\n    ],\n    \"3\": []\n  }\n}",
    "prompt_id": "mcr.reasoning_recall"
  },
