  "635caaa46aed3ecf6d89675c2a9d3303b5de3480a68d49d615d7e0731d57621b": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\n**Groundtruth Diagnostic Reasons (7 statements):**\n1. Ovarian cancer was suspected because of adnexal thickening and elevated CA-125 — “The radiologist suspected primary malignancy originating from thickening of bilateral adnexa which could be in the form of ovarian cancer.”\n2. A gynecologic origin was less likely after ultrasound showed no ovarian abnormalities — “There were no signs of abnormalities such as cancer or cysts on gynecological organs based on the USG.”\n3. Bone metastases were contemplated due to lytic, hypermetabolic bone lesions — “multiple osteolytic hypermetabolic metastases located in bone marrow of right humerus head, sternal bone region, several bilateral ribs, pelvic bone, and most of cervical/sacral vertebra bone.”\n4. Peritoneal carcinomatosis was considered from omental changes on PET — “presence of non-metabolic bleakness at the area of upper omentum with suspicion of metastatic seeding in the form of carcinomatosis omental.”\n5. Malignancy was questioned when fluid cytology showed no malignant cells — “Cytologic examination of pleural fluid showed lymphocytes, leukocytes, and mesothelial cells with no presence of malignant cells.”\n6. Tuberculous lymphadenitis was identified on lymph-node biopsy — “the biopsy showed tubercles surrounded by lymphocytes... the pathological anatomy report concluded the diagnosis as tuberculous lymphadenitis.”\n7. Abdominal tuberculosis was confirmed by laparoscopy and biopsy showing granulomas — “Omentum biopsy revealed granuloma containing epithelioid cells and Langhans-type cells. These results suggested tuberculosis infection of omentum and liver.”\n\n**Predicted Diagnostic Reasons (6 statements, labeled P1–P6):**\nP1: \"The patient's symptoms of worsening dyspnea, dry cough, fatigue, and significant weight loss, along with laboratory findings of elevated CA-125 and imaging results showing bilateral pleural and pericardial effusions, lytic lesions in bones, and FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa on PET-CT, are highly suggestive of ovarian cancer, particularly given the presence of hypermetabolic osteolytic lesions and lymph nodes.\"\nP2: \"The patient's presentation with dyspnea, dry cough, fatigue, weight loss, and abnormal labs (elevated CA-125, anemia) along with imaging findings (bilateral pleural effusions, pericardial effusion, lytic bone lesions, and FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa on PET-CT) suggests ovarian cancer with metastasis. The normal-sized ovaries on ultrasound do not rule out this diagnosis as ovarian cancer can present with metastatic disease even when the primary tumor is small.\"\nP3: \"The patient's presentation with worsening dyspnea, dry cough, fatigue, and significant weight loss, along with laboratory findings of elevated CA-125 and imaging findings of bilateral pleural and pericardial effusions, mediastinal lymphadenopathy, and lytic bone lesions, are highly suggestive of ovarian cancer, particularly given the PET-CT findings of FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa.\"\nP4: \"The patient has elevated CA-125 levels, bilateral adnexal thickening on PET-CT, and hypermetabolic lesions in multiple areas, which are suspicious for metastatic ovarian cancer. The lack of visible masses on ultrasound does not rule out this diagnosis, as some ovarian cancers can be difficult to detect by imaging.\"\nP5: \"The presence of lytic lesions in the sternum, right humerus, and thoracic vertebrae, along with hypermetabolic lymph nodes, pericardium, and pleura on PET-CT, indicates widespread metastatic disease, which is consistent with advanced ovarian cancer.\"\nP6: \"The presence of hypermetabolic osteolytic lesions in bone, hypermetabolic lymph nodes, pericardium, and pleura on PET-CT, along with the patient's clinical presentation and laboratory findings, support the diagnosis of metastatic disease, which is likely related to the primary ovarian malignancy.\"\n\n**Matching Analysis:**\n\n- **Groundtruth 1 (adnexal thickening + CA-125):**  \n  Potential matches: P1, P2, P3, P4 all explicitly mention \"FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa\" (or \"bilateral adnexal thickening\") and \"elevated CA-125\". P5 and P6 do not mention adnexal findings or CA-125.  \n  Pros: Directly address the same features. Cons: None.  \n  Decision: Match P1, P2, P3, P4.\n\n- **Groundtruth 2 (ultrasound no abnormalities, gynecologic origin less likely):**  \n  Potential matches: P2 and P4 mention ultrasound findings (\"normal-sized ovaries on ultrasound do not rule out\" and \"lack of visible masses on ultrasound does not rule out\"). However, these statements argue that ultrasound does not exclude ovarian cancer, whereas the groundtruth states that the gynecologic origin was *less likely*. The reasoning is opposite.  \n  Pros: Both mention ultrasound. Cons: Predicted statements dismiss the significance of normal ultrasound, while groundtruth uses it to reduce likelihood. Not equivalent justification.  \n  Decision: No match.\n\n- **Groundtruth 3 (bone metastases from lytic hypermetabolic lesions):**  \n  Potential matches: P1 (\"lytic lesions in bones\", \"hypermetabolic osteolytic lesions\"), P2 (\"lytic bone lesions\"), P3 (\"lytic bone lesions\"), P5 (\"lytic lesions in sternum, right humerus, thoracic vertebrae\"), P6 (\"hypermetabolic osteolytic lesions in bone\"). P4 does not specify bone lesions (only \"hypermetabolic lesions in multiple areas\").  \n  Pros: All five explicitly mention lytic or osteolytic bone lesions. Cons: None.  \n  Decision: Match P1, P2, P3, P5, P6.\n\n- **Groundtruth 4 (peritoneal carcinomatosis from omental changes):**  \n  Potential matches: None of the predicted statements mention omentum, peritoneal carcinomatosis, or omental changes.  \n  Decision: No match.\n\n- **Groundtruth 5 (fluid cytology no malignant cells):**  \n  Potential matches: None of the predicted statements mention pleural fluid cytology or cytology results.  \n  Decision: No match.\n\n- **Groundtruth 6 (tuberculous lymphadenitis on biopsy):**  \n  Potential matches: None of the predicted statements mention tuberculosis, lymph-node biopsy, or tubercles.  \n  Decision: No match.\n\n- **Groundtruth 7 (abdominal tuberculosis with granulomas):**  \n  Potential matches: None of the predicted statements mention granulomas, omentum biopsy, or tuberculosis.  \n  Decision: No match.\n\n**Summary of matches:**  \n- Groundtruth 1: P1, P2, P3, P4  \n- Groundtruth 2: none  \n- Groundtruth 3: P1, P2, P3, P5, P6  \n- Groundtruth 4: none  \n- Groundtruth 5: none  \n- Groundtruth 6: none  \n- Groundtruth 7: none  \n\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [\n      \"The patient's symptoms of worsening dyspnea, dry cough, fatigue, and significant weight loss, along with laboratory findings of elevated CA-125 and imaging results showing bilateral pleural and pericardial effusions, lytic lesions in bones, and FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa on PET-CT, are highly suggestive of ovarian cancer, particularly given the presence of hypermetabolic osteolytic lesions and lymph nodes.\",\n      \"The patient's presentation with dyspnea, dry cough, fatigue, weight loss, and abnormal labs (elevated CA-125, anemia) along with imaging findings (bilateral pleural effusions, pericardial effusion, lytic bone lesions, and FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa on PET-CT) suggests ovarian cancer with metastasis. The normal-sized ovaries on ultrasound do not rule out this diagnosis as ovarian cancer can present with metastatic disease even when the primary tumor is small.\",\n      \"The patient's presentation with worsening dyspnea, dry cough, fatigue, and significant weight loss, along with laboratory findings of elevated CA-125 and imaging findings of bilateral pleural and pericardial effusions, mediastinal lymphadenopathy, and lytic bone lesions, are highly suggestive of ovarian cancer, particularly given the PET-CT findings of FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa.\",\n      \"The patient has elevated CA-125 levels, bilateral adnexal thickening on PET-CT, and hypermetabolic lesions in multiple areas, which are suspicious for metastatic ovarian cancer. The lack of visible masses on ultrasound does not rule out this diagnosis, as some ovarian cancers can be difficult to detect by imaging.\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [\n      \"The patient's symptoms of worsening dyspnea, dry cough, fatigue, and significant weight loss, along with laboratory findings of elevated CA-125 and imaging results showing bilateral pleural and pericardial effusions, lytic lesions in bones, and FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa on PET-CT, are highly suggestive of ovarian cancer, particularly given the presence of hypermetabolic osteolytic lesions and lymph nodes.\",\n      \"The patient's presentation with dyspnea, dry cough, fatigue, weight loss, and abnormal labs (elevated CA-125, anemia) along with imaging findings (bilateral pleural effusions, pericardial effusion, lytic bone lesions, and FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa on PET-CT) suggests ovarian cancer with metastasis. The normal-sized ovaries on ultrasound do not rule out this diagnosis as ovarian cancer can present with metastatic disease even when the primary tumor is small.\",\n      \"The patient's presentation with worsening dyspnea, dry cough, fatigue, and significant weight loss, along with laboratory findings of elevated CA-125 and imaging findings of bilateral pleural and pericardial effusions, mediastinal lymphadenopathy, and lytic bone lesions, are highly suggestive of ovarian cancer, particularly given the PET-CT findings of FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa.\",\n      \"The presence of lytic lesions in the sternum, right humerus, and thoracic vertebrae, along with hypermetabolic lymph nodes, pericardium, and pleura on PET-CT, indicates widespread metastatic disease, which is consistent with advanced ovarian cancer.\",\n   