  "2003687d5b170ffa46e1b5dec31ba4b94ccb2a4f0ec9348aba5f28d84ec2bc38": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. \"Cranial nerve palsy\" — “This scan would also rule out other possible causes such as nerve palsy and schwannoma.”\n2. \"Schwannoma\" — “This scan would also rule out other possible causes such as nerve palsy and schwannoma.”\n3. \"Thyroid eye disease\" — “The globes were found to be unremarkable with no measured proptosis.”\n4. \"Orbital mass\" — “Noted is a right orbital linear soft tissue lesion … with enhancement pattern and signal intensity characteristics following extraocular muscle on all sequences.”\n5. \"Intracranial mass lesion\" — “There were no suprasellar or parasellar mass lesions.”\n6. \"Congenital syndromes (Duane syndrome, Brown’s syndrome, CFEOM)\" — “Clinical manifestation of an accessory extraocular muscle frequently occurs in childhood, along with numerous other congenital causes, such as Duane syndrome, Brown’s syndrome, and CFEOM.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons (two statements):\n- Statement A (from Diagnosis: Superior Oblique Muscle Palsy): \"The patient has a history of diplopia and an elevation deficit of the right eye, which suggests a problem with the extraocular muscles. The presence of a right orbital linear soft-tissue lesion medial to the medial rectus muscle, with signal-intensity characteristics identical to those of extraocular muscle, may indicate an anomalous or fibrotic muscle that could be contributing to the patient's symptoms.\"\n- Statement B (from Diagnosis: Orbital Fibrosis): \"The presence of a linear soft-tissue lesion in the right orbit, medial to the medial rectus muscle, with signal-intensity characteristics identical to those of extraocular muscle, suggests a fibrotic process. The history of previous surgery and lack of improvement in motility also supports this diagnosis.\"\n\nNow, for each Groundtruth statement:\n\nGT1 (Cranial nerve palsy): Predicted statements do not mention nerve palsy. They discuss extraocular muscle problems and a lesion, but not specifically cranial nerve palsy. No match. Pros: None. Cons: No reference to nerve palsy. Decision: No match.\n\nGT2 (Schwannoma): Predicted statements do not mention schwannoma. No match.\n\nGT3 (Thyroid eye disease): Predicted statements do not mention thyroid eye disease, proptosis, or globes. No match.\n\nGT4 (Orbital mass): Both predicted statements describe a right orbital linear soft-tissue lesion with signal-intensity characteristics identical to extraocular muscle. This directly matches the groundtruth description of a right orbital linear soft tissue lesion with enhancement pattern and signal intensity following extraocular muscle. Pros: Both explicitly mention the lesion location and signal characteristics. Cons: The groundtruth mentions \"enhancement pattern\" while predicted says \"signal-intensity characteristics identical to those of extraocular muscle\", but the meaning is equivalent. Also, the groundtruth includes \"Noted is a right orbital linear soft tissue lesion …\" which is essentially the same. Decision: Both Statement A and Statement B match GT4.\n\nGT5 (Intracranial mass lesion): Predicted statements do not mention intracranial masses or suprasellar/parasellar regions. No match.\n\nGT6 (Congenital syndromes): Predicted statements do not mention Duane, Brown, CFEOM, or congenital causes. No match.\n\nSummary of matching decisions:\n- GT1: no match\n- GT2: no match\n- GT3: no match\n- GT4: matches Statement A and Statement B\n- GT5: no match\n- GT6: no match\n\nJSON output will have keys 1-6, with key 4 containing a list of the two full predicted statements verbatim.\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": [\n      \"The patient has a history of diplopia and an elevation deficit of the right eye, which suggests a problem with the extraocular muscles. The presence of a right orbital linear soft-tissue lesion medial to the medial rectus muscle, with signal-intensity characteristics identical to those of extraocular muscle, may indicate an anomalous or fibrotic muscle that could be contributing to the patient's symptoms.\",\n      \"The presence of a linear soft-tissue lesion in the right orbit, medial to the medial rectus muscle, with signal-intensity characteristics identical to those of extraocular muscle, suggests a fibrotic process. The history of previous surgery and lack of improvement in motility also supports this diagnosis.\"\n    ],\n    \"5\": [],\n    \"6\": []\n  }\n}\n```",
