  "eb26e7978be88b16c01b16f61a4c3d51da3b091a565229e364841c2b6b07a514": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\n1. Groundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n   - GT1: Starvation ketosis was initially considered because of the patient’s week of poor oral intake and “clinical signs of failure to thrive the differential diagnosis initially included starvation ketosis.”\n   - GT2: Metformin-associated lactic acidosis was entertained given an elevated lactate level, but “the severe acidosis and altered mentation initially observed on presentation was unlikely to be solely due to lactic acidosis as severe lactic acidosis carries a very high mortality risk.”\n   - GT3: Euglycemic diabetic ketoacidosis was ultimately diagnosed as “euDKA is usually a diagnosis of exclusion” in a patient with SGLT-2 inhibitor use, normoglycemia, high anion gap metabolic acidosis, and marked ketonemia.\n\n2. Predicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n   - P1: The patient has a history of type 2 diabetes mellitus, and the laboratory results show severe metabolic acidosis, elevated anion gap, and elevated β-hydroxybutyrate level, which are consistent with diabetic ketoacidosis. The presence of trace ketones in the urine and elevated lactate level also support this diagnosis.\n   - P2: The patient has a history of type 2 diabetes mellitus, and the laboratory results show severe metabolic acidosis, elevated anion gap, and elevated β-hydroxybutyrate level, which are consistent with diabetic ketoacidosis. The presence of trace ketones in the urinalysis and the elevated lactate level also support this diagnosis.\n   - P3: The patient's laboratory results show acute renal failure with a significant increase in creatinine level from baseline, which indicates a decline in renal function. The patient's dehydration and severe metabolic acidosis may have contributed to the development of acute kidney injury.\n\n3. Matching assessment:\n   - GT1 vs P1/P2/P3: No predicted statement mentions starvation ketosis, poor oral intake, or failure to thrive. No match.\n   - GT2 vs P1/P2/P3: No predicted statement mentions metformin-associated lactic acidosis, altered mentation, or the mortality risk of severe lactic acidosis. Elevated lactate is mentioned, but only as supporting DKA, not as part of a lactic acidosis differential. No match.\n   - GT3 vs P1/P2: P1 and P2 both support DKA using the same key findings of severe metabolic acidosis, elevated anion gap, elevated β-hydroxybutyrate, and ketones. They do not mention SGLT-2 inhibitor use, normoglycemia, or “diagnosis of exclusion,” but the core laboratory justification for DKA/euDKA is present. Match.\n   - GT3 vs P3: P3 discusses acute kidney injury, not DKA. No match.\n\n4. Final matching decisions:\n   - GT1: no match\n   - GT2: no match\n   - GT3: P1 and P2 match\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [\n      \"The patient has a history of type 2 diabetes mellitus, and the laboratory results show severe metabolic acidosis, elevated anion gap, and elevated β-hydroxybutyrate level, which are consistent with diabetic ketoacidosis. The presence of trace ketones in the urine and elevated lactate level also support this diagnosis.\",\n      \"The patient has a history of type 2 diabetes mellitus, and the laboratory results show severe metabolic acidosis, elevated anion gap, and elevated β-hydroxybutyrate level, which are consistent with diabetic ketoacidosis. The presence of trace ketones in the urinalysis and the elevated lactate level also support this diagnosis.\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```",
