  "a36c70d2a270248c561f9b31900a33ddc790c9e65309837a6930aebf0edfc64d": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Osteoma considered because the lesion appeared benign on initial imaging — “The mass was suspected to be an osteoma of the temporal bone; however, the differential diagnosis also included osteochondroma and chronic sequela of prior trauma.”\n2. Osteochondroma considered as part of the surface bone–tumor differential — “The mass was suspected to be an osteoma of the temporal bone; however, the differential diagnosis also included osteochondroma and chronic sequela of prior trauma.”\n3. Chronic sequela of prior trauma considered given the history of a hyperostotic lesion — “the differential diagnosis also included … chronic sequela of prior trauma.”\n4. New-onset pain and increase in size argued against a longstanding benign lesion — “Considering the new onset pain and subjective increase in the size of the mass, the decision was made to proceed with excision.”\n5. Low signal intensity on both T1- and T2-weighted MRI suggested a low-grade surface osteosarcoma rather than a high-grade or dedifferentiated variant — “Low-grade variants will demonstrate low-signal intensity on transverse relaxation time on T1 … and T2 … weighted images. This is in contrast to high-grade variants which may show high T2 signal intensity areas.”\n6. Absence of soft-tissue extension or dural involvement made other malignant or metastatic processes less likely — “There is no soft tissue component present beyond the bone. The lesion does not involve the dura or any other intracranial structures.”\n7. Normal laboratory studies argued against systemic disease or infection — “Laboratory investigations were performed before the surgery and were found to be within normal limits.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons (two evidence statements):\nA: \"The patient's presentation of a hyperostotic lesion of the outer table of the right calvarium with dense calcification and low signal intensity on T1- and T2-weighted images, along with 